const partThreeSpecific = [
  [/what do older people and young people have in common/i, "People of different generations share basic needs such as security, friendship and feeling useful. Many enjoy spending time with family and want their work or hobbies to matter. They may use different technology or have different routines, but those differences do not remove common concerns. A good conversation often reveals more shared interests than people expect."],
  [/how can young people learn from older people/i, "Young people can learn from older adults' experience with work, relationships and setbacks, especially how they handled choices before today's technology was available. That does not mean every piece of advice will suit current circumstances. Asking follow-up questions helps distinguish a lasting lesson from a detail that has changed. Learning works both ways, because younger people can also share new skills and perspectives."],
  [/what can we learn from history/i, "History shows that societies change through a mix of decisions, conflicts, technology and ordinary people's responses. It can help explain why current borders, customs or institutions exist, and warn us when familiar problems appear again. We should not assume the past repeats exactly, though. Comparing sources and looking at context helps people draw lessons without treating history as a simple instruction manual."],
  [/what are the benefits of working for an international company/i, "Working for an international company can expose employees to different markets, customers and ways of organising work. That may create opportunities to build specialist skills or move between regions. It can also mean long hours across time zones and the challenge of communicating across cultures. It suits people who want that variety, although a local company may offer a closer connection to its community."],
  [/when do most children begin to have their own opinions/i, "Children start to express preferences when they are very young, but their ability to explain and compare opinions develops gradually through school age. Parents and teachers can influence them, yet a child's view should be heard in decisions that affect daily life. Adults should consider age and safety while giving children more say as their judgement grows."],
  [/whose opinions are more important to children/i, "Neither parents' nor children's opinions should automatically dominate. Adults have more experience and responsibility for safety, but children understand their own interests and feelings. For a small choice, such as a hobby, the child should usually decide; for health or safety, parents may need the final say. Discussing the reason helps children feel respected and learn to make decisions."],
  [/why do some people dislike being interviewed/i, "Some people dislike interviews because they feel judged, struggle to talk about themselves or have had an unfair experience. The pressure can be worse when questions are vague or the interviewer gives no feedback. A clear structure and respectful tone help, but nervousness is normal. For job applications, practising with real examples can help someone answer naturally instead of memorising a script."],
  [/what kinds of people are usually interviewed/i, "Interviews are common in job recruitment, journalism, research and healthcare. Employers speak with candidates, reporters interview people connected to a story, and researchers ask participants about their experiences. The purpose changes who is suitable: a journalist may want a witness, while a company needs someone with relevant skills. Interviewers should explain why they are asking questions and how the information will be used."],
  [/some people think pets should not be kept in cities/i, "Keeping pets in cities can be difficult when homes are small and there is little safe outdoor space. Noise, allergies and abandoned animals are also concerns. However, a well-cared-for pet can provide companionship, and city living is not automatically unsuitable if owners have time, suitable housing and access to parks or vets. The key issue is responsible ownership, not simply whether a person lives in a city."],
  [/who prefers travelling abroad\? the young or the old/i, "Younger adults may travel abroad more because they often have fewer family responsibilities and enjoy exploring new places. Older people may travel too, especially after retirement when they have more free time. Health, income and personal preference matter more than age alone. I would not say one group always travels more; the opportunity and reason for the trip make a big difference."],
  [/what makes a business fail/i, "A business often fails when it has no steady demand, poor cash-flow control or a product that does not solve a real problem. Strong competition can expose those weaknesses, especially if owners expand before they understand their customers. A small restaurant, for example, may attract interest at first but struggle with rent and waste. Planning and adapting matter as much as the original idea."],
  [/is it easy to start up a company/i, "Starting a company is more accessible than it used to be because people can test an idea online and reach customers without renting a large shop. Still, turning that into a stable income is difficult. Owners need funding, legal knowledge, time and a clear customer base. I would say it is relatively easy to begin, but much harder to keep the business healthy."],
  [/is it difficult to do business in today.s world/i, "It can be difficult because businesses face competition, changing customer habits and rising costs. Online platforms give small firms access to more buyers, but they also compete with companies from everywhere. Regulations and supply problems vary by industry, so there is no single experience. A business has a better chance if it understands a specific market and can adjust when conditions change."],
  [/what personal skills are required to work in an international company/i, "People need clear communication, adaptability and the ability to work with colleagues whose cultural expectations may differ. Language skills help, but listening and checking that everyone has understood a decision are just as important. An international team may work across time zones, so reliability matters too. Technical knowledge gets someone started; cooperation helps them contribute effectively over the longer term."],
  [/what goals should a society have/i, "A society should aim for health, safety, equal access to education and a reasonable standard of living. Economic growth matters, but it should not come at the cost of clean air or leaving some groups behind. Reliable public transport, for example, can connect people to jobs while reducing congestion. Progress is strongest when ordinary people can share its benefits."],
  [/what goals do people at your age have/i, "People in their early twenties often aim to finish their education, find stable work and become more financially independent. Some also want to travel, build relationships or develop a particular skill. These goals differ according to family circumstances and interests. I am finishing my mathematics degree, so finding a first job that lets me keep learning is one of my main priorities."],
  [/what kinds of noises are there in our life/i, "Everyday noise includes traffic, construction, alarms, loud music and conversations in shared spaces. Some sounds are useful, like a warning signal, while constant drilling or traffic can make it hard to concentrate or sleep. People's tolerance varies; someone who works nights may be especially bothered by daytime construction. Better planning and considerate behaviour can reduce unnecessary noise without trying to make cities silent."],
  [/what kinds of facilities do big cities have/i, "Large cities usually have extensive public transport, hospitals, universities, shopping centres, parks and cultural venues. These services can make daily life convenient, especially for people who need specialist care or a range of job options. They are not always easy to access, though; long journeys and high demand can make a nearby service feel crowded. Good planning matters as much as the number of facilities."],
  [/which is more helpful, watching videos or reading books/i, "Videos are more helpful when someone needs to see a process, such as how to use equipment or perform a practical skill. Books are better for detailed explanations that readers may want to revisit or compare. I use videos for a quick demonstration, but I prefer textbooks for a difficult mathematical idea because I can follow the reasoning at my own pace. The subject and purpose decide which format works better."],
  [/what makes a video go viral online/i, "A video is more likely to spread when it is easy to understand, emotionally engaging or useful enough for people to share. A funny clip or a short explanation of a surprising fact can travel quickly through group chats. Timing and platform recommendations also matter, so popularity does not always reflect quality. I think the most shareable videos give viewers a clear reason to pass them on."],
  [/why do some people not like using apps/i, "Some people dislike apps because they find the menus confusing, worry about privacy or get tired of constant notifications. Others prefer a website or speaking to a person, especially when a service is important and they need clear help. The problem is not always age; a poorly designed app can frustrate anyone. Simple layouts, readable text and a non-digital alternative make services easier to use."],
  [/what technology do young people like to use/i, "Young people commonly use smartphones, laptops and wireless headphones for study, messaging and entertainment. A phone is convenient for maps and quick updates, while a laptop is more comfortable for writing assignments or using specialised software. Preferences vary, of course; some enjoy gaming equipment or digital cameras. Most choose technology that fits their routine, rather than buying every new device that appears."],
  [/on what occasions do people like to make friends/i, "People often make friends when they meet repeatedly through school, work, clubs or shared hobbies. A party or trip can start a conversation, but seeing someone again gives the relationship time to develop. Starting university is a common example because students take classes and join activities together. The occasion helps, but a shared interest and regular contact usually matter more than one first meeting."],
  [/what festivals are the most important for people in your country/i, "The Spring Festival is probably the most important celebration for many people in China because families often travel home, share a meal and mark the beginning of a new year. Mid-Autumn Festival is also significant, particularly as a time to see relatives and eat mooncakes. Customs differ between regions and households. These festivals matter not only for the food, but for the chance to reconnect with family."],
  [/some people don.t mind others walking into their shot/i, "Some photographers do not mind a passer-by entering the frame because it can make a street picture feel spontaneous. Others prefer an uncluttered image, especially when they are composing a careful shot. The setting matters too: someone should not walk into a private or sensitive moment without permission. I would notice what the photographer is doing and avoid blocking the view if it is easy to do so."],
  [/when do people like taking photos/i, "People often take photos during holidays, family celebrations, meals with friends or when they see an unusual view. A phone makes it easy to record small moments as well as major events. Some people also photograph everyday details, such as a meal they cooked or a pet doing something funny. The best time is whenever a picture helps someone remember or share a moment without interrupting it."],
  [/do you think it is important to live in a community where people have healthy lifestyles/i, "It can help because neighbours influence what feels normal. If there are safe paths, sports areas and shops selling fresh food, people have more chances to make healthy choices together. Social support can also encourage someone to keep a routine. Still, people need privacy and different habits, so a healthy community should offer options rather than pressure everyone to live in exactly the same way."],
  [/are there any differences between the food people eat today and the food people ate in the past/i, "Yes. People today have wider access to food from different countries and can order meals quickly, while previous generations relied more on seasonal ingredients and home cooking. Work patterns have also changed how often families eat together. Traditional dishes have not disappeared, but convenience foods are more common. The difference is partly about choice and partly about how much time people have."],
  [/do you think it is good to change one.s daily routine/i, "A routine is worth changing when it no longer supports someone's needs. For example, studying earlier in the day may help a student who is too tired to focus late at night. Changes do not need to be dramatic; trying one adjustment for a week gives useful evidence. If a routine already works well, there is no reason to replace it just for novelty."],
  [/is there any food in your country that is eaten at special times or on special occasions/i, "Dumplings are often eaten during the Spring Festival in many parts of China, although customs vary between families and regions. Preparing them can be a shared activity, and the meal gives relatives time to catch up. Other celebrations have their own dishes, such as birthday noodles. The food matters partly because of its taste, but also because people associate it with a particular gathering."],
  [/do people often change their plans/i, "Yes, plans often change when the weather, work or other people's schedules shift. Students may move a group meeting because someone has a deadline, for instance. A plan is helpful, but it should not become so rigid that a small change causes stress. I try to keep the main goal clear and adjust the timing when new information makes the original arrangement impractical."],
  [/would you tell others if you change your plan/i, "Yes, especially if the change affects their time or preparation. I would explain as soon as I knew, rather than wait until the last minute, and suggest another option if possible. For a casual personal plan, a short message is enough. In group work, I would also clarify which tasks need to be reassigned so everyone knows what to do next."],
  [/do people need to have goals/i, "Goals can give people direction and help them decide how to use their time. A student might set a goal to finish a degree or improve a skill, then break it into manageable steps. Goals should be flexible, though; circumstances and interests can change. I think they are useful when they guide choices without making people feel that one missed target means they have failed."],
  [/is it good for a person to be ambitious/i, "Ambition can motivate someone to learn, take responsibility and keep going after a setback. It becomes unhealthy when success matters more than relationships, health or fair treatment of others. A person can aim high while staying open to a different route. I admire ambition most when it is connected to genuine interest and steady effort, rather than only a desire for status."],
  [/what kind of music events do people like today/i, "Many people enjoy large concerts by popular singers, but smaller live-house shows, music festivals and community performances also attract audiences. Online platforms help listeners discover artists and decide which event suits them. Younger people may be interested in current pop acts, while others prefer classical or traditional music. The choice often depends on ticket prices, location and whether someone wants a lively crowd or a quieter performance."],
  [/what are the differences between old and young people.s music preferences/i, "Young people may follow current pop, hip-hop or electronic music through short videos, while older listeners often return to songs they heard when they were younger. That is a general pattern, not a rule; taste depends on personality and social circles. Music can also bring generations together when a family shares a song. New styles appear, but people of every age enjoy both familiar and unfamiliar sounds."],
  [/what kind of music events are there in your country/i, "China has concerts by pop singers, classical performances, traditional opera and smaller local music events. Large venues host well-known performers, while university societies and live houses give emerging musicians a chance to play. The options differ between major cities and smaller towns. People can also watch performances online, but a live audience creates a shared atmosphere that a recording cannot fully reproduce."],
  [/what are the differences between listening to music at home and at a live concert/i, "At home, people can choose the volume, pause the music and listen whenever it suits them. A concert offers stronger sound and the energy of a crowd, so even familiar songs can feel new. It also costs more and requires travel, whereas home listening is convenient. I would listen at home most days, but go to a concert for an artist I particularly like."],
  [/what kinds of jobs need imagination/i, "Imagination is useful in design, advertising, research and teaching, but it can help in almost any job. A scientist may picture a different explanation for a result, while a designer imagines how people will use a product. Creativity still needs practical limits and evidence. It is not only about producing art; it also helps people find a new approach when a familiar method is not working."],
  [/what subjects are helpful for children.s imagination/i, "Art and music give children space to experiment, while science and language lessons can also encourage them to ask questions and invent explanations. The teaching method matters more than the subject title: an open-ended task invites more ideas than copying a single answer. Children benefit from having time to try something, make mistakes and revise it. A mix of subjects helps them see problems from different angles."],
  [/what games help develop children.s imagination/i, "Building blocks, role-play and drawing games can all encourage imagination because children make choices and invent their own rules. A simple set of blocks might become a bridge, a shop or a space station. Some video games also allow players to design worlds, although too much screen time is not ideal. The important part is leaving room for a child to create rather than only follow instructions."],
  [/how important is imagination to children/i, "Imagination helps children explore possibilities, practise empathy and solve problems in a playful way. When they invent a story, they have to think about what different characters might do. It can also make learning more engaging. Children still need facts and routines, but creative play gives them a safe space to test ideas. Adults can encourage it by asking questions instead of correcting every unusual answer."],
  [/why do people like to visit historical sites/i, "Historical sites let people see details that are difficult to picture from a textbook, such as the scale of a building or how a street was laid out. Visitors may also feel connected to local stories and earlier generations. A guide or museum display can make the visit more meaningful. Some people go for architecture or photographs, but the strongest impression often comes from understanding how the place was used."],
  [/why are some people well-rounded and others only good at one thing/i, "People become well-rounded when they have chances to try different subjects and activities, while others focus deeply on one area because it interests them or suits their goals. Family expectations, school opportunities and time all play a role. Neither path is automatically better. Broad experience can help people connect ideas, while specialisation builds expertise; many careers need a mix of both."],
  [/why does modern society need talents of all kinds/i, "Modern society faces problems that cannot be solved by one kind of expertise. Engineers may design a system, but communities also need teachers to explain it, artists to communicate ideas and organisers to make it work. Different strengths help teams see risks that one specialist might miss. A society benefits when it values practical, creative and caring skills instead of treating only a few careers as important."],
  [/why do people prefer to watch movies in the cinema/i, "People choose the cinema for its large screen, strong sound and the feeling of watching with an audience. It can make an action or science-fiction film more absorbing than a phone or laptop. Going out also turns the film into a social event. The disadvantages are ticket prices and fixed showtimes, so many people save cinema visits for films that feel worth seeing on a big screen."],
  [/do you think good teachers are always popular among students/i, "No. A good teacher can be respected without being the most popular person in class. Clear explanations, fairness and genuine attention to students matter more than trying to be liked. Some teachers set demanding work, which students may not enjoy at the time, but still value later. Popularity can make a classroom comfortable, though it is not a reliable measure of teaching quality."],
  [/is it important for a teacher to be popular/i, "It is helpful if students feel comfortable speaking to a teacher, but popularity should not be the main goal. A teacher needs to explain clearly, treat students fairly and set appropriate expectations. Some lessons are challenging, so not everyone will enjoy them. I think trust and respect are more important than being popular, because they make students more willing to ask questions and accept feedback."],
  [/is it important to have the same hobbies and interests when making friends/i, "Shared interests make it easier to start a conversation and spend time together, but they are not essential for a lasting friendship. People can enjoy different hobbies and still share values such as honesty or humour. In fact, a friend with different interests can introduce you to something new. What matters more is whether both people listen and make an effort to stay connected."],
  [/do you think people in the countryside are friendlier than people in the city/i, "I would not say one group is naturally friendlier. In a small community, people may recognise their neighbours more often, while city residents have more interactions with strangers and may keep to themselves. That can create different habits rather than different personalities. Whether people are welcoming depends on the situation and the individuals, so I would avoid judging friendliness by where someone lives."],
  [/what are the advantages and disadvantages of advertisements/i, "Advertisements help people learn that a product or service exists, and they can make it easier to compare prices or features. They also fund many free websites and programmes. The downside is that repeated or misleading adverts can pressure people into buying things they do not need. Clear claims and rules against deceptive marketing make advertising more useful to customers."],
  [/what is the most important factor in an advertisement/i, "The most important factor is a clear, believable message. People should be able to understand what the product does and who it is for without being distracted by dramatic effects. A short demonstration or specific piece of evidence can build more trust than a famous face. If the advert makes exaggerated claims, viewers may remember it but still decide not to buy anything."],
  [/why are some advertisements boring/i, "Some adverts are boring because they repeat the same message, use familiar images or interrupt people when they are focused on something else. They may also be aimed at the wrong audience, so the product has no relevance to the viewer. An advert is more engaging when it tells a small story or shows a practical use. It still needs to be brief and honest."],
  [/which is more effective, online advertising or offline advertising/i, "Online advertising can target people based on their interests and measure clicks, while television, posters or public transport adverts reach people who are not actively searching. The better option depends on the product and audience. A local shop may benefit from signs nearby, whereas a specialist service could use online search. Good advertising still needs a clear message, whatever the channel."],
  [/do many people grow vegetables or flowers at home in your country/i, "Yes, some people grow herbs, flowers or vegetables on balconies and in small community gardens, especially when they enjoy cooking or gardening. It is more difficult for people in apartments with little sunlight, so not every household can do it. Even a few pots of spring onions can be useful. People often value the routine and fresh produce as much as saving money."],
  [/is it easy to grow plants at home/i, "It can be easy if someone starts with plants suited to the amount of light and space they have. Herbs such as mint or spring onions do not need a large garden, but they still need regular watering. Beginners may lose a plant while learning what it needs. A simple pot is a manageable start, and experience makes it easier to notice problems early."],
  [/is it easy for children in cities to get close to the natural world/i, "It depends on where they live. Children with a park, school garden or safe walking route nearby can see plants and insects regularly, but some dense neighbourhoods have little green space. Families may need to travel to a larger park at weekends. Schools can help by organising outdoor activities, although city planning also needs to make nature accessible in everyday life."],
  [/are rivers and lakes important to a country/i, "Yes, they provide water, support farming and wildlife, and can be used for transport or recreation. Some rivers have also shaped settlement and trade over many generations. Their importance varies by region, but pollution or drought can affect a large number of people. Protecting water quality is therefore a practical concern for public health and the economy, as well as for nature."],
  [/do you think people should spend more time in nature/i, "For many people, yes, because time outdoors offers a break from screens and gives them room to walk or relax. It does not need to involve a long hike; a nearby park can be enough. However, people have different schedules and access, so it should not become another expectation. Cities can make it easier by providing safe, pleasant green spaces close to where people live."],
  [/will there be more or less green space in cities in the future/i, "It could go either way. Growing populations create pressure to build housing and roads, which may reduce open land, but many cities are adding pocket parks, rooftop gardens and tree-lined routes. The result depends on planning choices and whether green space is protected when new development is approved. I hope cities treat parks as essential infrastructure rather than decoration that can be removed later."],
  [/do you think parents should teach their children how to protect the environment/i, "Yes, parents can make environmental care part of ordinary routines, such as sorting waste, saving water and respecting animals outdoors. Children are more likely to follow those habits if adults explain why they matter and do the same themselves. Parents should also show that responsibility is shared; families can make small choices, while companies and governments need to address larger sources of pollution."],
  [/do you think rivers and lakes attract tourists/i, "Yes, clean rivers and lakes attract people who want to enjoy scenery, go boating or spend time outdoors. They can also support local restaurants and guesthouses. The attraction depends on access and water quality; polluted banks or overcrowded paths can put visitors off. Local authorities need to manage litter and protect habitats so tourism does not damage the natural feature people came to see."],
  [/should people be very kind when they help others/i, "People should be considerate, but helping does not mean taking over or ignoring what the other person wants. It is better to ask what support would be useful and respect their answer. For example, a classmate may need someone to explain one step rather than finish an assignment for them. Kindness works best when it protects the other person's dignity and independence."],
  [/should children help their parents with household chores/i, "Yes, children can do age-appropriate chores such as putting away toys, setting the table or sorting laundry. Sharing these tasks helps them understand that keeping a home running is everyone's responsibility. Parents should show them how and avoid expecting a young child to manage an adult workload. The aim is cooperation and practical skills, not using chores as a punishment."],
  [/should children ask their teachers or parents for advice when making plans/i, "Yes, especially for decisions that affect school, safety or family schedules. Parents understand the child's needs, while teachers may know more about academic choices. Children should be able to hear both views and explain what they want. Adults can point out consequences without making every decision for them, so the child gradually learns how to plan independently."],
  [/should people prepare before giving advice/i, "Yes, they should understand enough about the situation before offering an opinion. Advice based on one assumption can make a problem worse, even when the intention is kind. Asking a few questions first helps reveal what the person has already tried and what they actually need. It is also useful to make advice optional, because the person facing the decision has the most context."],
  [/do you think being a doctor is easy or difficult/i, "It is a demanding job because doctors need years of training and have to make careful decisions under pressure. They also speak with people who may be frightened or in pain, so communication matters as much as medical knowledge. Some parts are rewarding, particularly when a patient improves, but long shifts and emotional responsibility can be difficult. The experience also varies by specialty and workplace."],
  [/do you think doctors should be paid more/i, "Doctors should receive fair pay that reflects their training, responsibility and often long hours. Whether salaries should rise across the board depends on public budgets and differences between specialties. Pay alone will not solve problems such as staff shortages or heavy workloads. Governments should also improve working conditions and make sure doctors in less popular regions have support, so patients can access care."],
  [/is it good to have a daily routine/i, "A routine is useful because it reduces the number of small decisions people need to make and helps them protect time for sleep, study or exercise. My weekdays are easier when I know when I will work on coursework. A routine can become restrictive if every hour is fixed, though. It should provide structure while leaving room for rest and unexpected plans."],
  [/is talent important in sports/i, "Talent can give an athlete an early advantage, but practice, coaching and discipline are essential for long-term performance. Someone may have natural speed yet struggle if they do not train consistently or work well with a team. Access to good facilities also matters. I think talent helps people start, while effort and support often determine how far they progress."],
  [/does art, like music or theater, have an impact on society/i, "Art can influence society by giving people a way to express experiences and discuss issues they might otherwise avoid. A play or song can make an audience see an event from another person's perspective. It does not change policy by itself, but it can shape public conversation and preserve culture. Its effect is strongest when people can access and interpret it for themselves."],
  [/is it hard to choose a gift/i, "It can be difficult when you do not know what the person needs or when you are worried about getting the choice wrong. I find it easier when I remember something they mentioned or choose an experience we can share. The price is less important than showing that you paid attention. A gift card can also be sensible if the person prefers choosing for themselves."],
  [/is smiling important in your culture/i, "A smile is a common way to show warmth or politeness, especially when greeting someone or thanking them. Still, people do not smile in every situation, and a serious expression is not necessarily unfriendly. The meaning depends on context and personality. I think being respectful matters more than displaying a particular expression, although a genuine smile can make a first meeting feel easier."],
  [/do you prefer to prepare and organize an activity or just take part in an activity/i, "I usually prefer helping to organise a small activity because I like making sure people know where to go and what to bring. For a larger event, I am happy just to take part because planning can become a lot of work. It depends on my time and interest. I enjoy organising most when tasks are shared and everyone can contribute."],
  [/why do people get up early/i, "People get up early for different reasons, including work, school, exercise or a long commute. Some also find the morning quiet and use it for focused tasks before the day becomes busy. It is partly a matter of habit and sleep pattern, not simply discipline. Someone who works late may be more productive later in the day, so early rising does not suit everyone."],
  [/what kinds of occasions need people to arrive early/i, "People should arrive early for occasions where missing the start would cause a problem, such as an exam, a job interview or an international flight. It gives them time to find the right place and deal with unexpected delays. For a casual meal with friends, arriving exactly on time is usually enough. How early to be depends on the consequences of being late."],
  [/why do some people like to stay up late/i, "Some people stay up late because work or study takes longer than expected, while others enjoy having quiet time after family responsibilities are finished. Their natural sleep patterns can also differ. Staying up late may feel relaxing, but it becomes a problem if it regularly reduces sleep and affects concentration the next day. A consistent schedule is more useful than forcing everyone to sleep at the same time."],
  [/what kind of people like getting up early/i, "People who start work early, have a long commute or enjoy morning exercise may prefer getting up early. Some feel more alert in the morning and like finishing important tasks before distractions begin. Others are naturally more energetic later in the day. I do not think early risers are automatically more productive; the best schedule is one that fits someone's responsibilities and lets them sleep enough."],
  [/what can people do to become more organized/i, "People can become more organised by writing down deadlines, breaking large tasks into steps and deciding what needs attention first. I keep a short list for coursework rather than trying to plan every hour. Reviewing it at the end of the day helps me see what is still unfinished, even during a busy week. The system works only if it stays simple enough to use."],
  [/why is it important for people to be organized/i, "Being organised saves time and reduces the chance of forgetting an important commitment. It also makes a large task feel more manageable because people can see the next step instead of worrying about everything at once. In a group project, clear roles prevent classmates from duplicating work. A routine is helpful, but it should leave room for unexpected changes."],
  [/how can parents help children to be organized/i, "Parents can help by keeping a predictable routine and showing children how to prepare for the next day. A child could pack a school bag before bed and use a simple checklist for homework. Adults should give reminders at first, then let the child take more responsibility. The aim is to build a habit, not to control every detail of a child's schedule."],
  [/on what occasions do people need to be organized/i, "People need to be organised when several tasks depend on one another, such as preparing for an exam, travelling or running an event. A wedding or university presentation can involve deadlines, bookings and other people's schedules, so missing one detail affects everyone. Everyday routines need less planning. I would put more effort into preparation when mistakes would be costly or difficult to fix."],
  [/how would you feel when you were not well prepared for something/i, "I would feel anxious at first, especially if other people were depending on me. I would try to work out what was essential and deal with that before worrying about the parts I could no longer change. Afterwards, I would note what I had overlooked. Being unprepared is uncomfortable, but reviewing it can help me plan more realistically next time."],
  [/what should people wear to a job interview/i, "People should wear something neat and appropriate for the workplace, rather than choosing an outfit just to look expensive. A formal company may expect a jacket, while a creative studio could be more relaxed. Clean, comfortable clothes also help applicants focus on the conversation. I would check the company's usual style beforehand and choose something that still feels natural to wear."],
  [/why do we need to attend interviews when applying for jobs/i, "An interview lets an employer hear how a candidate explains their experience and responds to questions, which a written application cannot fully show. It also gives the applicant a chance to ask about the role, team and expectations. It should be a two-way conversation, not just a test. A clear process helps both sides decide whether the job is a sensible fit."],
  [/what should people do to prepare for a job interview/i, "People should learn about the role, review the examples on their CV and prepare a few questions for the employer. Practising answers aloud can make it easier to explain a real experience clearly, but memorising a script may sound unnatural. They should also check the time and route in advance. Good preparation reduces avoidable stress and leaves more attention for the actual conversation."],
  [/why do some people dislike being interviewed/i, "Some people dislike interviews because they feel they are being judged in a short time, often while trying to manage nerves. The format can also seem unfair if questions are vague or interviewers give little feedback. A candidate may have strong practical skills but find it difficult to describe them under pressure. Clear questions and a respectful tone make the process more useful for everyone."],
  [/what kinds of people are usually interviewed/i, "People are interviewed when their experience or opinion can help explain a subject. Employers interview job applicants, while journalists may speak to experts, witnesses or people directly affected by an event. Public figures are interviewed because audiences are curious about their decisions. The interviewer should explain the purpose and avoid presenting one person's story as if it represented everyone with a similar experience."],
  [/what do people often feel after losing things/i, "People often feel frustrated or worried after losing something, especially if it contains money or personal information. They may replay their recent movements and blame themselves, which does not always help. It is better to check likely places calmly and contact the venue. If a bank card or phone is missing, protecting the account should come before finding the object itself."],
  [/what can we do to reduce the possibility of losing things/i, "A simple routine helps: keep keys and cards in the same place, check a seat before leaving and avoid carrying more than necessary. Labels or a tracking function can help with valuable items, although privacy settings matter. I try to put my student card in one pocket every time. Small habits are more reliable than assuming we will remember everything when we are rushed."],
  [/what kinds of people may lose things often/i, "Anyone can lose things, but it is more likely when someone is tired, rushing or trying to manage several tasks at once. People may also misplace belongings in crowded places where they keep moving between queues or seats. I would not describe them as careless by nature; a busy day can distract almost anyone. Slowing down briefly before leaving often makes a difference."],
  [/what will people do when losing things/i, "Most people retrace their steps and check the places they visited most recently. If they lost something in a shop or station, they can ask staff or contact lost property. For a phone or bank card, they should lock it or cancel access quickly. Posting online may help, but they should avoid sharing personal details that could let someone misuse the item."],
  [/what kinds of things do people usually lose/i, "People commonly lose small everyday items such as keys, travel cards, earphones and umbrellas because they take them in and out of bags. Phones and wallets are less easily replaced, so losing them causes more concern. Items are often left on public transport or in cafes when someone is distracted. Keeping essentials in one consistent pocket or compartment makes them easier to check."],
  [/does money equal success/i, "No, money can provide security and choices, but it is only one measure of success. Someone may earn a high salary yet have little time for family or work they find meaningful. I would also consider whether a person is healthy, has supportive relationships and feels their work contributes something useful. Financial stability matters, but it does not automatically create a satisfying life."],
  [/is it easier to spend money or save money/i, "For many people it is easier to spend because the reward is immediate, while the benefit of saving comes later. Online shopping also makes small purchases very convenient. Saving becomes easier when someone sets a clear goal and transfers a manageable amount regularly. I find it helps to wait a day before buying something non-essential, so I can decide whether I still want it."],
  [/do you think it is important to save money/i, "Yes, saving gives people a buffer for unexpected costs and more freedom to make future choices. A student might save for a laptop, while a family may need money for rent or medical bills. The amount depends on income, so advice should be realistic rather than make people feel guilty. Even a small regular contribution can be useful over time."],
  [/do people like to spend their leisure time out in your country/i, "Many people in China enjoy spending free time outside the home, especially by eating with friends, visiting a shopping centre or walking in a park. These activities are easy to arrange and suit different budgets. Still, some people prefer staying home after a demanding week. The choice depends on work hours, weather and whether someone wants company or quiet time."],
  [/do many people keep pets in your country/i, "Yes, cats and dogs are common pets in China, particularly in cities. People enjoy the companionship, and many treat pets as part of the family. Apartment living can make it harder to keep a large dog, so owners need to think about space, exercise and local rules. A pet is a long-term responsibility, not just something pleasant to have at home."],
  [/is it important to teach students environmental protection at school/i, "Yes, school is a good place to explain how everyday choices connect with larger environmental problems. Students can learn why waste sorting matters, how ecosystems support people and what actions are actually effective. A clean-up or small garden project makes the subject concrete. Lessons should avoid suggesting individuals can solve everything alone; governments and businesses also have responsibility for pollution and resource use."],
  [/do you think air pollution comes mostly from mobile vehicles/i, "Vehicles are an important source of air pollution in busy cities, but they are not the only one. Power generation, factories, construction and heating can also produce emissions, depending on the region and season. Traffic is visible, so people may notice it first. Better public transport and cleaner vehicles help, but governments need to measure all major sources before deciding where policy will have the greatest effect."],
  [/do you think people need to change the way of transportation drastically to protect the environment/i, "People need to use cleaner transport more often, but change should be practical rather than abrupt. Reliable buses, safe cycle lanes and walkable neighbourhoods give people real alternatives to driving. Some rural residents have no convenient public transport, so private vehicles may remain necessary. Governments can encourage gradual changes while improving the infrastructure first; asking people to change without offering another option is unlikely to work."],
  [/do you think it.s a waste of money for governments to celebrate national events/i, "Not necessarily. Public celebrations can bring people together and help younger generations understand national history, but the spending should be proportionate. A large event is difficult to justify if local services are underfunded or the cost is hidden. Governments could use public parks, schools and community groups rather than expensive displays. The value depends on whether people benefit and whether the budget is transparent."],
  [/do you think governments should put a large amount of money into medical research/i, "Governments should fund medical research because its results can improve prevention and treatment for many people, including those who cannot afford private care. The work may take years, so stable public funding matters. However, projects should be reviewed openly and include common health needs, not only profitable treatments. Research spending is worthwhile when it is carefully managed and its findings are shared responsibly."],
  [/do people in your country usually obey the law/i, "Most people follow ordinary laws, particularly when the rules are clear and enforcement seems fair. People are more likely to respect traffic or public safety rules when they can see how those rules protect others. Some may ignore regulations they consider unreasonable or inconsistently applied. Education and accessible ways to comply usually work better than punishment alone, although serious offences still require proper consequences."],
  [/do you think governments should provide financial support to companies/i, "Governments can support companies when the help protects jobs, encourages useful research or helps an industry change to cleaner practices. The support should have clear conditions and a time limit, otherwise public money may simply protect an unsuccessful business. Small firms may need access to loans or training more than a direct grant. Any programme should publish who receives funding and what results it achieves."],
  [/do you still keep in touch with your friends from childhood/i, "Yes, I still message one or two friends from childhood, although we do not meet as often as we used to. We can talk about old memories without explaining every detail, which makes the friendship feel familiar. Our studies and work have taken us in different directions, so staying close requires some effort. Even an occasional call helps us keep that connection."],
  [/do young people prefer spending time with family or with friends/i, "It depends on the person and the stage of life. Young people may spend more time with friends because they share classes, hobbies and similar routines, while family offers support and a sense of belonging. During holidays, they may choose both. I do not think one relationship should replace the other; having different kinds of support is valuable."],
  [/do you think people who like to smile are more friendly/i, "A smile can make someone seem approachable, but it does not prove they are friendly. Some people smile because they feel nervous or want to be polite, while others are warm but have a more serious expression. I would pay more attention to how someone listens and treats people over time. First impressions matter, but they are only a small part of judging character."],
  [/do you think it.s good to communicate when eating with your family/i, "Yes, mealtimes can be an easy chance for family members to catch up without arranging a separate meeting. Even a short conversation about the day helps people stay connected. It should not become compulsory, though; someone may need quiet after work or study. I think putting phones aside for part of the meal can help, as long as the conversation remains relaxed."],
  [/do you think online communication through social media will replace face-to-face communication/i, "I do not think online communication will completely replace meeting in person. Messages and video calls are convenient when friends live far apart, but face-to-face conversations give people more cues, such as tone and body language. Many relationships use both: a quick message keeps in touch, while meeting in person helps people discuss something important. Technology changes how we connect, but it does not remove the value of shared time."],
  [/does technology help people save time/i, "Technology saves time when it automates repetitive tasks or makes information easier to find. Online banking and digital calendars are good examples. It can also create distractions through notifications or require time to learn a new system. I use spreadsheets for calculations that would take longer by hand, but I still check the results. The benefit depends on whether the tool actually simplifies the task."],
  [/do you think technology has changed the way people communicate/i, "Yes, people can now send a message or share a photo instantly, even across countries. That makes it easier to maintain friendships, but short messages can miss tone and encourage people to reply without giving full attention. I use group chats for practical updates and call a close friend for a serious conversation. The technology has changed the speed and format more than the need to communicate."],
  [/do you think it is necessary to have laws on the use of mobile phones/i, "Some rules are necessary when phone use can put people at risk, especially while driving or crossing a busy road. Schools and workplaces may also limit phones during tasks that need concentration. A total ban in every setting would be excessive, because phones are useful for emergencies and learning. Rules should target the situation, explain the reason and allow sensible exceptions."],
  [/do you think staying in a natural place would help change people.s mood/i, "Yes, being somewhere green can improve people's mood because it gives them a break from noise, screens and routine. Even a short walk through a park can help someone feel calmer after a difficult day. The effect is not identical for everyone, and access matters; a safe, well-maintained space is more inviting than a neglected one. Nature can support wellbeing, though it cannot replace treatment for serious problems."],
  [/what kind of people do you think enjoy being in nature/i, "People who want quiet or a change of pace often enjoy nature, but it appeals to more than hikers. Families may choose a park because children can move around, while photographers notice light and scenery. In my case, a riverside walk is a simple way to step away from study. What they enjoy depends on the activity and how easy the place is to reach."],
  [/what environmental laws does your country already have/i, "China has rules on air pollution, waste sorting and protecting wildlife, although enforcement can differ between places. In larger cities, residents may be asked to separate household rubbish, and factories face limits on emissions. Such rules matter most when inspections and public information are consistent. Legislation alone cannot solve environmental problems, but it gives authorities a basis to prevent damage and hold polluters responsible."],
  [/what laws about the environment are effective in your country/i, "The most effective environmental laws are clear, enforceable and aimed at major sources of damage. Limits on industrial emissions can make a measurable difference when factories are monitored and violations lead to meaningful penalties. Waste rules are useful too, but people need convenient collection systems. I would judge a law by whether air, water or recycling outcomes improve, rather than by how strict it sounds on paper."],
  [/which do you think people prefer, rewards or punishment, when it comes to government intervention in environmental protection/i, "I think a mix works better than either one alone. Rewards can encourage households or businesses to adopt greener habits, particularly when the change takes effort at first. Serious pollution still needs penalties because a company should not gain financially by ignoring the rules. The government should make the standards clear and apply them consistently. Incentives build cooperation; enforcement protects the public when voluntary action is not enough."],
  [/what can people do to protect the natural world/i, "People can reduce waste, use public transport where possible and avoid disturbing wildlife, but individual action has limits. Local groups can organise clean-ups or report damage, while residents can support businesses that use fewer disposable materials. Governments and companies have greater influence over infrastructure and production, so responsibility should be shared. Small habits matter most when services make the sustainable choice practical and affordable."],
  [/how do rivers and lakes affect local tourism/i, "Rivers and lakes attract visitors for walks, boat trips, fishing and views, so nearby cafes and guesthouses may benefit. A clean waterfront can also become a public space that residents use every day. However, heavy tourism can create litter, noise and pressure on wildlife. Local authorities need to manage access and waste carefully; otherwise the attraction may be damaged by the very visitors it brings."],
  [/how can rivers and lakes benefit local people/i, "Rivers and lakes provide water for households and farming, support wildlife and can give communities places to exercise or relax. Some are also important for transport, fishing or tourism, depending on the region. The benefits depend on water quality, so pollution can quickly affect people's health and local livelihoods. Protecting the river is practical as well as environmental; residents, businesses and authorities all need to keep waste out of it."],
  [/why are many advertisements endorsed by celebrities/i, "Companies use celebrities because a familiar face can attract attention and make a product seem more appealing. Fans may also trust a person they admire, even though that does not mean the celebrity has tested the product carefully. A famous endorsement is useful for awareness, but clear information and honest demonstrations matter more if a company wants lasting trust. I still compare products rather than buying something just because of the person in the advert."],
  [/how useful are they/i, "Celebrity endorsements can be useful for attracting attention, especially when the person is a natural fit for the product. A well-known athlete can make running shoes memorable, for example. Still, a familiar face cannot prove that the shoes are comfortable or durable, so customers need clear product details and independent reviews. I see endorsements as a way to raise awareness, not as evidence of quality."],
  [/is advertising important for a company.*\bwhy\b/i, "Because companies compete for people's attention and customers have many alternatives. Advertising helps a business reach the right audience, explain its value and become recognisable. It can also remind existing customers about a product. However, promotion only supports a good business; if the product disappoints or the advert overpromises, people are unlikely to buy again."],
  [/is advertising important for a company/i, "Yes, advertising matters because it tells potential customers that a product exists and explains what makes it different. Without it, even a useful product may remain unknown. A clear advert can also help a company reach the people most likely to need what it offers. It works best when its claims are honest, since misleading promotion can damage trust and future sales."],
  [/do older people prefer spending time with family or with friends/i, "It varies, but family often becomes especially important when older people need practical support or regular company. Friends matter too: people of a similar age may share long memories and interests without needing much explanation. Distance, health and personality can affect the balance. I think most older adults value both, even if they spend more time with one group."],
  [/how do people decide when to travel/i, "People often choose travel dates around work or school breaks, weather, cost and who is available to go with them. Families may prefer school holidays, even though flights and hotels are then more expensive. Someone with flexible work can travel in a quieter season. The best time depends on the destination; checking local weather and events can prevent an otherwise good trip from being uncomfortable."],
  [/how do people choose a city to travel to/i, "People usually compare what they want to do with practical details such as transport, cost and how long they have. Someone interested in history may choose an older city, while a family may look for safe attractions and convenient hotels. Reviews help, but they can make popular places seem more appealing than they are. I would check both the main sights and ordinary neighbourhoods before deciding."],
  [/why do more people own and drive private vehicles now/i, "Private vehicles are more common because rising incomes make them affordable to more families, and they offer flexibility when public transport is limited. A car is convenient for carrying children or shopping, while motorbikes suit short trips through crowded streets. The downside is congestion and pollution, especially when many people drive at the same time. Reliable public transport can reduce the need to own one."],
  [/what do you think of the future of electric cars/i, "Electric cars are likely to become more common as batteries improve and charging points spread. They can reduce exhaust pollution in cities, although their overall environmental impact also depends on how electricity and batteries are produced. For drivers, price, charging time and access to a home charger will matter as much as range. I expect gradual growth rather than every household switching immediately."],
  [/why do you think some people choose not to travel abroad/i, "Some people avoid travelling abroad because it can be expensive, time-consuming or difficult to plan around work and family. Others may feel uncomfortable with language differences or prefer exploring their own country first. Not travelling does not necessarily mean they dislike other cultures; it may simply be a practical choice. International travel is enjoyable, but it is not essential for everyone."],
  [/why do some children enjoy making things by hand/i, "Children enjoy making things by hand because they can turn an idea into something they can touch and show to others. The process also gives them room to experiment without needing a perfect result. For example, a child might change the shape of a paper model after it falls over. Simple materials keep it accessible, and praise for effort can make them more willing to try again."],
  [/how do people learn new things/i, "People learn best through a mix of explanation, practice and feedback. Watching a short demonstration can introduce a new skill, but trying it themselves helps them notice what they do not yet understand. I remember mathematical ideas better when I work through a problem rather than only reread the proof. The right balance depends on the subject and learner, but active practice is hard to replace."],
  [/what is the most important thing for learning a language well/i, "Regular practice is probably the most important factor because language needs to be used, not just memorised. Learners need to listen, speak, read and get feedback over time, even if each session is short. I find that noting a phrase from a podcast and trying it in conversation helps it stick. Confidence matters too; people improve faster when they are willing to make small mistakes."],
  [/what can teachers do to stop students from relying too much on ai/i, "Teachers can set tasks that require students to explain their reasoning, check sources and connect ideas to class discussions, rather than submit an AI answer unchanged. They can teach students to compare the tool's claims with reliable material and discuss where it may be wrong. A blanket ban is hard to enforce. Clear rules and assessment of the student's process encourage responsible use."],
  [/how important is it for schools to help children become smarter/i, "Schools can help children develop their abilities by offering challenging work, useful feedback and different ways to explore a subject. Being smart is not only about getting high marks; curiosity, persistence and communication matter too. A student who finishes early could investigate a harder problem or explain a method to peers. Support should stretch children without making them feel their worth depends on always being the best."],
  [/do you think most people.s decisions are made in their 20s/i, "People make important choices in their twenties, such as what to study, where to work and whether to move, but many decisions happen earlier or later as well. This decade often brings more independence, so choices can strongly affect daily life. Still, few decisions are irreversible. People can change careers or priorities, which is why I would not call one decade decisive for everyone."],
  [/do you think people like the process of making plans more, or the moment of carrying them out/i, "Some people enjoy planning because it lets them imagine possibilities and feel prepared; others prefer acting, since progress becomes visible only once they begin. I like making a basic plan for study, but I get more satisfaction from finishing the work. Planning can become an end in itself if someone keeps adjusting details. A useful plan should make action easier, not delay it."],
  [/do you think children sometimes have to make important decisions/i, "Yes, children sometimes need to make decisions about friends, activities or how to respond when something feels unfair. Adults should guide them and explain likely consequences, but making every choice for them prevents them from learning. The level of responsibility should match their age; a child can choose an after-school club, while a serious health or safety decision needs adult involvement. Practice builds confidence gradually."],
  [/are there any changes in your city/i, "Cities change as populations grow and people's needs shift. New housing, shops and transport links can make daily life more convenient, while older areas may be renovated or replaced. In my city, new buildings have appeared around busy roads, but green space has not always kept up. Change is useful when it improves residents' lives and preserves the places that give a neighbourhood its identity."],
  [/do people.s ideal jobs change as they grow up/i, "Yes, people's idea of an ideal job often changes with age and experience. A teenager may focus on interest or status, while someone supporting a family might place more weight on stable hours and income. Work experience can reveal what kind of environment suits them. That does not mean people give up their ambitions; they may redefine success to fit their responsibilities and values."],
  [/do you think it.s important to plan ahead/i, "Planning ahead is useful for commitments that involve time, money or other people. It helps someone see what needs to happen first and avoid leaving important tasks until the last minute. A group assignment needs early agreement on roles so one person is not left doing everything. Small everyday choices do not need detailed plans, though; flexibility is valuable when circumstances change."],
  [/what activities do we need to plan ahead/i, "People should plan ahead for activities involving bookings, travel, deadlines or several people's schedules. A group project, for example, needs clear roles and check-in dates so the final week is not chaotic. Everyday tasks like choosing lunch rarely need much preparation. I think planning is most useful when forgetting one step would affect other people or make the activity more expensive."],
  [/is it important to protect historic buildings/i, "Yes, historic buildings are worth protecting when they help people understand a city's past or have architectural value. They can also give neighbourhoods a distinctive character. Preservation costs money, so not every old structure can be kept exactly as it is. Careful restoration and a useful new purpose can make a building part of modern life instead of leaving it empty."],
  [/do you think there should be many natural places in cities/i, "Cities should include parks and other natural spaces because residents need places to walk, exercise and get a break from traffic and buildings. Trees can also provide shade and help manage rainwater. The spaces do not all need to be large; small gardens and riverside paths can serve nearby communities. Planning them early is easier than trying to add green space after an area is fully built."],
  [/is it worth spending a lot of money on the exterior appearance of a building/i, "A building's exterior matters because it affects the street and can make a public space more welcoming, but spending a very large amount only for decoration is hard to justify. Safety, accessibility and long-term maintenance should come first. A thoughtful design can be attractive without using expensive materials. The budget should reflect how the building will be used and how many people benefit from it."],
  [/is it more important for a building to look good on the outside or on the inside/i, "The inside is more important because people need rooms that are comfortable, safe and practical every day. The outside still matters when a building shapes a public street or represents a cultural place. Ideally, the design works well in both ways, but appearance should not come at the expense of ventilation, accessibility or useful space. A building is successful when people enjoy using it, not only photographing it."],
  [/do you think it.s reasonable to charge an entry fee for visiting interesting buildings/i, "An entry fee can be reasonable when it pays for maintenance, staff and conservation, especially at a historic site. It should remain affordable, and students or local residents could receive discounts. Some public buildings should be free because they provide a community service. The decision depends on the site's costs and purpose, but visitors should be able to see clearly how the money supports the place."],
  [/do you think the high ways will help reduce traffic jams/i, "A new highway can reduce congestion on a particular route by giving drivers another way through, but it may also encourage more people to use cars over time. That can move the traffic problem elsewhere. Highways help most when they connect to a wider transport plan and avoid busy residential areas. Better public transport and sensible land use are usually needed as well."],
  [/do you like to use public transport/i, "Yes, I like public transport when it is frequent and easy to navigate. I can read or listen to something during the trip instead of watching traffic, and it is usually cheaper than driving. Crowded buses at rush hour can be uncomfortable, so service quality matters. For most city journeys, a reliable bus or metro is a practical choice for both passengers and the environment."],
  [/why do some people like to remodel and decorate their homes themselves/i, "People may decorate their home themselves to save money, express their taste or make the space fit their daily routines. Doing the work can also be satisfying, especially when they see a clear result. Some tasks require professional skills, though, and a poor repair may cost more later. Many people do small jobs themselves and hire an expert for electrical or structural work."],
  [/what do chinese people do when they visit others/i, "When visiting friends or relatives in China, people often bring fruit, snacks or a small gift, especially for a first visit. They may share tea or a meal and spend time catching up with the host. Customs differ by family and region, so visitors usually follow the host's lead. The main purpose is to show care and strengthen the relationship, not to follow a strict ceremony."],
  [/what kind of place do people in your country like to live in/i, "Many people in China prefer a home that is close to work, public transport, shops and schools. In cities, apartments are common because they are more available and easier to maintain than a large house. Some families would rather have a quieter neighbourhood or more outdoor space, even if it means a longer commute. The right place depends on household size, budget and daily routines."],
  [/why do some people prefer to live in the countryside/i, "Some people prefer the countryside because it is quieter, offers more space and keeps them close to nature or family. The cost of housing may also be lower. The trade-off is fewer local jobs and services, so transport and internet access matter. A rural home suits people whose work and lifestyle fit the area; it is not automatically a better choice for everyone."],
  [/what do people need to remember in their daily lives/i, "People need to remember appointments, deadlines, household tasks and small details such as keys or travel cards. A calendar or a consistent place for important items can reduce the effort of keeping everything in mind. I use reminders for university deadlines but avoid recording every minor task. The aim is to support memory, not fill the day with alerts that become easy to ignore."],
  [/why do some young people like to live in cities/i, "Young people often choose cities because they offer universities, varied jobs, public transport and places to meet friends. Living nearby can make it easier to try different careers and activities. The disadvantages are high rent, crowds and a faster pace. Some people later move to a smaller place when their priorities change. City life is attractive when its opportunities justify the cost and pressure."],
  [/in your country, do people nowadays cook at home as frequently as people did in the past/i, "People in China may cook at home less often than previous generations because many work long hours and can order food quickly. At the same time, some young adults are learning to cook for health or budget reasons, and families still prepare meals together on weekends. The pattern varies by age and city. Convenience has changed routines, but home cooking remains important for many households."],
  [/why do most children think education is boring/i, "Some children find school boring when lessons feel disconnected from their interests or they mostly copy information without taking part. The same subject can become engaging with a practical example or a chance to ask questions. Students also differ in how they learn, so one teaching style will not suit everyone. A clear purpose and appropriate challenge can make lessons feel more worthwhile."],
  [/what can people do when they feel bored/i, "People can change activity, go for a short walk, call a friend or start a small task they have been putting off. The best choice depends on whether they need rest or stimulation. I sometimes leave my desk for a few minutes after studying and return with a clearer head. Boredom can also be a sign that someone needs more variety or social contact in their routine."],
  [/when do you think people are happiest/i, "People often feel happiest when they have close relationships, a sense of security and some control over how they spend their time. A particular age does not guarantee that; children may have fewer responsibilities, while adults can make more independent choices. Small daily moments, such as a good conversation or finishing a task, also matter. Happiness usually comes from a combination rather than one achievement."],
  [/how can people relieve stress in daily life/i, "People can reduce everyday stress by taking a short break, exercising, talking to someone they trust or dividing a large task into smaller steps. Sleep and a predictable routine also help. I find that a brief walk clears my head when university work piles up. These habits cannot remove every cause of stress, so people may also need to change an unrealistic workload or ask for support."],
  [/what effect does stress have on health/i, "Short-term stress can help someone focus before an exam or deadline, but constant stress may disrupt sleep, affect mood and make concentration harder. People may also develop headaches or neglect exercise when they are under pressure for a long time. The effect varies between individuals. Rest, social support and manageable routines can help, but persistent symptoms deserve proper medical advice rather than being ignored."],
  [/what kinds of things make people feel pressured/i, "People often feel pressured by deadlines, financial responsibilities, family expectations and the sense that they must keep up with others. Students may face several assignments at once, while parents balance work and care. Pressure is not always harmful; a clear short-term goal can motivate someone. It becomes a problem when demands continue without enough control, rest or support."],
  [/why do some people think interviews are a waste of time/i, "Some people see interviews as a waste of time when questions are repetitive or the employer has already made a decision. Applicants may spend hours travelling or preparing and then receive no feedback. A well-run interview is still useful because both sides can discuss expectations and working style. Clear questions and a prompt response make the process feel respectful and worth the effort."],
  [/what are the rules people should obey at work/i, "Employees should follow safety procedures, respect colleagues' time and protect confidential information. They also need to meet reasonable deadlines and communicate early if a problem affects their work. Exact rules vary by job, so employers should explain them clearly during training. A fair workplace applies the same standards to everyone and allows staff to raise concerns when a rule is unsafe or impractical."],
  [/what is the purpose of punishment/i, "Punishment is intended to discourage harmful behaviour and show that rules have consequences. It may also protect other people in the short term. However, punishment alone does not explain why an action was wrong or how to behave differently next time. For children and minor mistakes, a proportionate response with a clear explanation can teach more than a severe penalty. The goal should include prevention and learning."],
  [/what can governments do to encourage people to spend more time in nature/i, "Governments can make nature easier to reach by protecting parks, adding safe walking and cycling routes and improving public transport to green spaces. Free community activities, such as guided walks, can also encourage people who are not used to spending time outdoors. It is important to maintain the areas and keep them accessible to older people and families. People are more likely to visit when the space feels safe and welcoming."],
  [/what kinds of behavior are considered as good behavior/i, "Good behaviour usually means treating others with respect, keeping promises and considering how one's actions affect people nearby. In a shared space, that might mean waiting your turn or speaking at a reasonable volume. Expectations differ between cultures and settings, so politeness is not always expressed in the same way. The common idea is to show consideration without expecting people to behave identically."],
  [/what are the benefits for people to obey rules/i, "Following reasonable rules can protect people's safety, reduce conflict and make shared services work more fairly. Traffic rules, for example, help drivers and pedestrians predict what others will do. Rules also make expectations clearer, which is useful at school or work. They should still be reviewed when they create unnecessary barriers. People are more willing to follow rules when they understand their purpose and see them applied consistently."],
  [/why do some people refuse to abide by rules/i, "People may ignore rules when they see them as unfair, confusing or impossible to follow. Others take risks because they assume the rule will not be enforced or because convenience matters more in the moment. Clear explanations and practical ways to comply can improve cooperation. Enforcement may be necessary, but it works best alongside trust; a rule that treats people inconsistently is likely to lose respect."],
  [/what should the government do to improve citizens. safety/i, "Governments can improve safety by maintaining roads and public spaces, enforcing fair laws and making emergency services easy to reach. They should also use reliable data to identify risks, such as dangerous crossings, and consult the people who use those places. Safety measures need regular review so they do not restrict people's rights unnecessarily. Good design and prevention can often solve a problem before punishment is needed."],
  [/what can young and old people learn from each other/i, "Older people can share experience about work, relationships and dealing with setbacks, while younger people may help them understand new technology or current social changes. Both sides benefit when they ask questions rather than assume the other has nothing to offer. A grandparent learning to use a video call, for example, may hear about a grandchild's life more often. The exchange should be mutual and respectful."],
  [/which group pays more attention to health, young people or old people/i, "Older people may pay closer attention to health because they have more experience with illness and regular check-ups. Younger people may focus on fitness or appearance, but their habits vary widely. Work, income and access to healthcare also affect how much attention someone can give. Rather than compare generations, it is more useful to make healthy food, exercise and medical advice accessible at every age."],
  [/who tend to change their daily routine more, young people or old people/i, "Young people often change routines more because they move between education, new jobs and different social groups. Older adults may have a more settled schedule, although retirement or health changes can alter it significantly. These are general patterns, not rules. A person's responsibilities and circumstances matter more than age alone. Some routine is helpful, but everyone needs room to adapt when life changes."],
  [/some people say large cities are suitable for old people/i, "Large cities can suit older people when they have good hospitals, accessible transport and nearby shops, but crowds and high living costs may be difficult. Smaller towns can feel calmer and make it easier to know neighbours, though specialist services may be farther away. There is no single best place. Safety, family support and the older person's own preferences should guide the choice."],
  [/which is more suitable for young people, urban life or rural life/i, "Urban life often gives young people more universities, jobs and cultural activities, while rural areas may offer lower costs, more space and closer community ties. The better choice depends on their career and lifestyle. Some young people prefer a city during study and move later for family or housing. Reliable internet and transport are making the distinction less absolute than it used to be."],
  [/what are the differences between the tv programs young people like to watch and those old people like to watch/i, "Young people often watch short online videos, reality shows and series they can follow on demand, while older viewers may prefer news, traditional dramas or programmes they watch at a fixed time. There is considerable overlap, especially when families watch together. The difference is partly about platform and habit, not only age. People choose content that matches their interests and how much time they have."],
  [/how do young and old people use mobile phones differently/i, "Young people often use phones for messaging, video, games and social media throughout the day. Older adults may focus more on calls, family photos and practical services, although many are comfortable with apps too. The difference often comes from what someone has learned and needs, rather than age itself. Clear design and patient support make it easier for everyone to use a phone confidently."],
  [/when would old people ask young people for advice/i, "Older people may ask younger relatives for help with new apps, online services or current trends, because younger people often use those tools more regularly. They might also ask about education or job choices when a family member has recent experience. Advice can go both ways, though. Older adults usually have practical knowledge of their own, so the best conversation shares expertise rather than treating one generation as the teacher."],
  [/do you think it.s ok to take photos everywhere/i, "No, people should not take photos everywhere without considering others. It is usually fine in a public park, but museums, private homes and some workplaces may have rules to protect privacy or valuable objects. People should ask before photographing someone closely, especially a child. A quick check of signs and the people around you is a simple way to avoid making others uncomfortable."],
  [/do you think people should learn ancient history before learning modern history/i, "Students do not need to study all ancient history before learning about modern events. Starting with a period that connects to their lives may help them become interested, while ancient history can explain the origins of later institutions and customs. A balanced course can move between periods and show how events relate. The order matters less than helping students understand context and compare different sources."],
  [/do you think children should begin learning history from an early age/i, "Children can begin learning history early through family stories, local places and simple accounts of how life used to be. They do not need to memorise dates before they can understand a timeline. A visit to an old building or a conversation with a grandparent can make the past concrete. More detailed debates about evidence can come later, as their reading and reasoning develop."],
  [/do you think people should read the original book before watching the movie/i, "No, people do not need to read the book first. A film can stand on its own, and watching it may encourage someone to read the original afterwards. Reading first gives more detail and lets people imagine the characters before seeing an actor's interpretation. I prefer whichever format suits my time and interest; comparing the two can be enjoyable, but it should not feel like homework."],
  [/do you think people today read less than before/i, "People may read fewer printed books, but they still read messages, articles and other material on screens every day. The bigger difference may be that attention is divided between short posts and longer writing. Some people read less for pleasure because work and entertainment compete for time. Libraries and digital books make reading accessible, so habits vary more than a simple decline suggests."],
  [/are there any unlucky things that people should not buy as gifts in your country/i, "People may avoid gifts that carry an unlucky meaning in Chinese culture, such as clocks for certain occasions, because the pronunciation can be associated with saying goodbye. Customs vary by family, so it is wise to consider the recipient rather than follow a rule blindly. A safe choice is something useful or personal. The thought behind the gift matters, but cultural sensitivity helps avoid an awkward moment."],
  [/will people feel happy when receiving an expensive gift/i, "An expensive gift can make someone feel appreciated, but it may also create pressure to return the favour or worry that the item is not suitable. People often value a gift more when it shows the giver understands their interests. I would rather receive something thoughtful that I can use than something costly chosen to impress. The relationship and intention shape the reaction more than the price."],
  [/do chinese people prefer to watch domestic movies or foreign movies/i, "Chinese audiences watch both domestic and foreign films, and their preference depends on genre, story and what is available in cinemas. Domestic films may feel culturally familiar and reflect local experiences, while foreign films can offer different settings and styles. A strong film can succeed regardless of where it was made. Friends' recommendations and online discussion also influence what people choose to watch."],
  [/are there many tall buildings in your country/i, "Yes, tall buildings are common in major Chinese cities, especially in residential and business districts where land is limited. They allow more people to live or work near transport and services. Smaller cities and older neighbourhoods still have lower buildings. A high-rise is practical only when lifts, fire safety and public space are planned well; otherwise density can make daily life uncomfortable."],
  [/do you think it is a good trend to have more big cities/i, "Larger cities can create more jobs, universities and specialist services, which attracts people looking for opportunity. But continuous growth also raises housing costs, traffic and pressure on green space. I do not think every place needs to become a megacity. A better goal is to develop several well-connected cities so people can find work and services without all moving to the same centre."],
  [/do you think there are some people who enjoy crowded places/i, "Yes. Some people enjoy the energy of a busy market, concert or shopping street, especially when they are with friends. Crowds can make an event feel lively and create a sense of shared excitement. Other people find them tiring because movement and noise are harder to manage. Whether a crowded place is enjoyable depends on the purpose, the person's temperament and whether they can leave when they want."],
  [/will people use bikes more or less in cities in the future/i, "People may use bikes more if cities provide protected lanes, secure parking and safe routes to public transport. Bikes are useful for short trips and can reduce congestion, but weather, distance and road safety affect people's choices. Electric bikes also make longer journeys easier. I expect cycling to grow in some areas, though it will work best alongside reliable buses and trains rather than replacing them."],
  [/do you think a tourist city is also a good place to live/i, "A tourist city can be a good place to live because it often has restaurants, transport and cultural events. The downside is that popular neighbourhoods may be crowded, noisy and expensive, especially during peak seasons. Residents need daily services and affordable housing, not only attractions. It can work well when city planners manage visitor numbers and make sure tourism supports local businesses and public spaces."],
  [/do you think the big cities in china today will become even larger in the future/i, "Some large Chinese cities may keep growing because they offer jobs, universities and transport links that smaller places cannot match. However, high housing costs and congestion could encourage people and companies to move to surrounding cities. Growth may happen across a region rather than only inside one centre. Planning transport and public services early will determine whether that expansion improves people's lives."],
  [/is it better to live in a new building or an old one/i, "A new building usually offers modern wiring, lifts and better insulation, while an older building may have a distinctive layout and a more established neighbourhood. The condition matters more than age: a well-maintained older home can be comfortable, and a poorly built new one can create problems. I would compare safety, transport, cost and natural light before deciding."],
  [/do you think developing public transport can solve traffic jam problems/i, "Better public transport can reduce traffic jams by giving commuters a reliable alternative to driving, especially during rush hour. It works best when buses and trains are frequent, affordable and connect easily with walking or cycling routes. It cannot solve every traffic problem because deliveries and some jobs require vehicles. Still, shifting everyday passenger trips away from private cars can make roads move more smoothly."],
  [/what do you think young people spend most of their money on/i, "Young people often spend money on food, transport, clothes, devices and social activities. Those still living with family may save more, while students living independently face rent and daily costs. Spending also depends on income and priorities. I think many are willing to pay for experiences with friends, but a budget helps them avoid letting convenience purchases take money away from longer-term plans."],
  [/what kind of things are people happy to pay a high price for/i, "People are willing to pay more for things they trust or use every day, such as a reliable phone, comfortable shoes or a good meal on a special occasion. Some also pay for experiences that feel memorable. A high price is not automatically good value, though. Buyers usually compare quality, durability and how much they will use the item before deciding it is worth the cost."],
  [/what kinds of movies do you think are successful in your country/i, "In China, films often do well when they combine an engaging story with themes audiences recognise, such as family, friendship or national history. Comedy and action are also popular because they offer a shared, entertaining experience. Marketing and release timing can help, but people still recommend a film when they enjoy it. Success can mean ticket sales, lasting discussion or both."],
  [/what are the factors that make a successful movie/i, "A successful movie needs a story that holds attention, convincing performances and pacing that suits its audience. Visual effects may attract people, but they cannot replace characters viewers care about. Distribution and promotion also matter because audiences need to hear about the film. A smaller film can succeed through strong word of mouth, while a large production may earn money without being remembered for long."],
  [/in your country, what industry is it easier to be successful in/i, "There is no single industry where success is easy, but technology, online retail, healthcare and education offer opportunities in China because demand is changing quickly. Each field also has strong competition and requires different skills. A person is more likely to do well when they understand a real need and keep learning, rather than choosing an industry only because it is currently popular."],
  [/why are some people willing to spend a lot of money on meals on special days/i, "People may spend more on a special meal because it marks a wedding, birthday or family reunion and gives everyone a chance to celebrate together. Choosing a good restaurant can make the occasion feel different from an ordinary day. Some also see an expensive meal as a way to show care, though that is not necessary. Shared time matters more than the price of the menu."],
  [/how do people spend their leisure time in your country/i, "People in China spend free time meeting friends, eating out, watching films, exercising, travelling nearby or relaxing with online entertainment. The choice depends on age, location and how much time someone has. Students may meet at a cafe after class, while families often choose a meal or a park at weekends. More people also use short periods of free time for hobbies at home."],
  [/why do people like to have days off/i, "Days off give people time to recover from work or study, spend time with family and do things that do not fit into a busy weekday. Rest can improve concentration when people return to their responsibilities. Some use a day off for travel or appointments, while others prefer staying home. A break is valuable even when it is ordinary, because people need time that is not controlled by deadlines."],
  [/in what situations do people spend a long time responding to others. messages|when do people spend a long time responding to others. messages/i, "People take longer to reply when a message needs a thoughtful answer, such as discussing a disagreement, giving advice or confirming plans with several people. They may also be busy at work or want time to check details before responding. A delay does not always mean they are ignoring the sender. For an urgent issue, a short call is usually clearer than waiting for a detailed text."],
  [/in what situations do people not respond to messages at all|in what situations do people not respond to messages right away/i, "People may not reply when they are driving, working, sleeping or spending time with someone in person. Some also miss a notification or do not know what to say, especially if a message feels difficult. If the matter is not urgent, it is reasonable to wait. For something time-sensitive, the sender can make that clear or use a more direct way to get in touch."],
  [/what would you do if you did not receive a reply after sending out a message|what would you do if you haven.t received a reply after sending out a message/i, "I would first give the person time, because they may be busy or have missed the notification. If I needed an answer, I would send one polite follow-up with the key detail rather than several messages. For an urgent matter, I would call. I try not to assume someone is upset with me until I have a reason, since people have different routines and response habits."],
  [/why do some people prefer sending a message instead of making a call/i, "People often text because they can send information without interrupting someone and the other person can reply when free. A message is also useful for sharing an address, link or time that needs to be checked later. A call is better for an urgent or emotional conversation, where tone matters. The choice depends on how quickly the answer is needed and how complicated the topic is."],
  [/how do you show your respect in your messages/i, "I show respect by being clear, using a polite tone and avoiding repeated messages when someone has not replied. I also try to choose a suitable time and give enough context so the other person understands what I need. For a formal message, I check names and details before sending. Those small habits show that I value the recipient's time and do not expect an instant response."],
  [/why do some people feel angry when others don.t reply to their message/i, "Some people feel ignored when a message is read but not answered, especially if they expected a quick response or were asking about something important. Social media can make people assume everyone is available all the time. The sender may not know what the other person is doing, though. Clear expectations help; people should allow reasonable time unless the message is genuinely urgent."],
  [/what goals do young people usually have/i, "Young people often aim to finish their education, find work they can develop in and become more independent. Some also want to travel, support their family or build a creative project. Goals vary with money, health and opportunity, so it is unfair to assume everyone wants the same career. A useful goal gives direction but can change as someone learns more about what suits them."],
  [/what should people do to achieve their goals/i, "People can make a goal manageable by turning it into smaller steps, setting a realistic schedule and checking progress regularly. If someone wants to work in data analysis, they might first improve spreadsheet skills and complete a small project. Feedback helps them adjust their plan. Persistence matters, but so does changing methods when they do not work. A goal should guide effort rather than become a source of constant pressure."],
  [/why should children have ambitions/i, "Ambitions can help children imagine possibilities and stay interested in learning, even if their plans change later. A child who wants to be an engineer might become curious about building and mathematics. Adults should encourage that curiosity without expecting the child to make a final career decision too early. Ambition is useful when it gives direction and hope, not when it becomes pressure to achieve a particular status."],
  [/what do you think of people going after high positions/i, "Seeking a senior position can be positive if someone wants greater responsibility and is prepared to develop the skills it requires. The role may also bring higher pay and a chance to influence decisions. However, status alone is a weak reason to pursue it, because management can involve stress and difficult conversations. People should consider whether the work itself suits them, not only the title."],
  [/why are some young people keen on being fans of superstars/i, "Young people may admire a superstar's talent, style or success and feel part of a community of fans. Following someone can also provide entertainment and a shared topic with friends. Social media makes it easy to see updates and join discussions. It becomes less healthy if a fan spends beyond their means or treats the celebrity's public image as a complete picture of their private life."],
  [/what can people do to save time/i, "People can save time by preparing what they need in advance, grouping similar errands and deciding which tasks matter most. I pack my notes before an early class so I do not search for them in the morning. Technology can help with reminders, but too many notifications create distraction. The best method is a simple routine that removes repeated decisions without making every part of the day rigid."],
  [/what can schools do to help students learn time management/i, "Schools can teach students to break a large assignment into stages, estimate how long each will take and set an earlier personal deadline. Teachers can show how to use a weekly planner and review it after a project. Students need practice, not just advice to 'be organised'. Schools should also coordinate major deadlines where possible, because time management cannot solve an unreasonable workload on its own."],
  [/what activities do we need to plan ahead/i, "People benefit from planning ahead for activities that involve other people, travel, deadlines or significant costs. A group trip needs transport and accommodation arranged early, while a university project needs time reserved for research and revision. Small daily tasks may not need a detailed plan. Planning is most helpful when a late decision would create stress or limit people's options."],
  [/what can schools do to help students learn time management/i, "Schools can teach students to break a large assignment into stages, estimate how long each will take and set an earlier personal deadline. Teachers can show how to use a weekly planner and review it after a project. Students need practice, not just advice to 'be organised'. Schools should also coordinate major deadlines where possible, because time management cannot solve an unreasonable workload on its own."],
  [/what kind of help do people need when looking for a new job/i, "People looking for a job often need practical feedback on their CV, interview practice and information about what the role is really like. Emotional support can help too, because repeated applications are tiring. A friend in the same field may know about openings, while a career adviser can give more neutral guidance. The most useful help is specific to the person's skills and next step."],
  [/why are employees reluctant to ask their managers for help/i, "Employees may hesitate because they worry a manager will see a question as a sign that they are not capable. Some managers are also difficult to approach or respond impatiently. A workplace can reduce that fear by making regular check-ins normal and treating early questions as a way to prevent bigger mistakes. Asking for help should be seen as responsible when someone has already tried to solve the problem."],
  [/why do some people visit galleries or museums instead of viewing artworks online/i, "Seeing art in person lets people notice its real size, texture and detail, which a screen may flatten. A gallery also gives visitors a quiet place to concentrate and compare works in the same setting. Online images are more convenient and let people explore collections from home, but they cannot fully recreate the experience. Many people use both: online to discover art and galleries to see it closely."],
  [/how do artworks inspire people/i, "Art can inspire people by showing a familiar subject from an unexpected point of view or expressing an emotion they recognise. A painting may make someone notice the colour and light in an ordinary street, while a sculpture can prompt questions about shape and space. People respond differently, so inspiration is personal. It can encourage someone to create something themselves or simply look at daily life more carefully."],
  [/what kind of job can be called a dream job/i, "A dream job is different for each person, but it usually combines work they find meaningful with conditions they can sustain. Some value a creative role, others want stability or time for family. For me, a job using data to solve practical problems sounds appealing because it connects with my mathematics degree. Salary matters, but a job should also suit someone's strengths and priorities."],
  [/what should people consider when choosing jobs/i, "People should consider what tasks they enjoy, what skills they have and whether the working conditions fit their lives. Salary and job security matter, but so do commute time, opportunities to learn and workplace culture. Someone choosing between two roles could speak to people already doing that work. A good decision balances practical needs with the kind of life the person wants outside work."],
  [/what short-term jobs do young people do in other countries|what kind of work can young people do in foreign countries/i, "Young people abroad may take seasonal work in tourism, hospitality, agriculture or language support, depending on their visa and skills. Some also join internships or short research placements related to their studies. These jobs can provide income and experience, but applicants should check the contract, accommodation and legal requirements. A short placement is most useful when the work offers learning as well as a chance to see another country."],
  [/what challenges do young people face when working abroad/i, "Young people working abroad may face a language barrier, unfamiliar workplace expectations and the stress of arranging housing or transport. Being far from family can also feel difficult when something goes wrong. A clear contract, a supportive supervisor and basic knowledge of local rules help. The first weeks may be demanding, but the experience can build confidence if the worker has a realistic plan and someone to contact for advice."],
  [/why are some people unwilling to work in other countries/i, "Some people do not want to work abroad because they would be far from family, have to adapt to another language or risk losing a stable job at home. Others may not see a clear career benefit for the disruption. Those concerns are reasonable, especially for people with care responsibilities. Working abroad suits some people, but it should be a genuine choice rather than something everyone is expected to do."],
  [/do most people prefer shopping at big stores or small stores/i, "Big stores are convenient because they offer a wider range and often lower prices, while small shops can provide personal service and local products. People may choose a mall for a planned shopping trip but use a neighbourhood shop for one or two items. Neither is always better. The choice depends on price, convenience and whether the shopper values a particular product or relationship with the seller."],
  [/are success books useful to everyone/i, "Success books can be useful when they offer practical ideas or examples that readers can adapt to their own lives. They are not a formula, because people's goals, resources and responsibilities differ. Some books also make success sound easier than it is. I would take one useful suggestion and test whether it works for me, rather than assume that following every step guarantees the same result."],
  [/do you think it is necessary to be ambitious when working in a team in a company/i, "Ambition can help an employee take responsibility and look for ways to improve, but it should not come at the team's expense. In a project, people need to share credit and help colleagues meet the common goal. Someone can be motivated without competing for every promotion. A healthy workplace recognises individual progress while making sure people cooperate and communicate openly."],
  [/do people who can manage time well become successful more easily/i, "Good time management makes it easier to meet commitments and protect time for important work, so it can support success. It is not enough on its own: people also need skills, opportunities and sometimes support from others. A student who plans revision can use study time more effectively, but an unrealistic schedule will not help. The useful habit is setting priorities and adjusting when circumstances change."],
  [/do you often buy more than you expected/i, "Occasionally, especially when I buy groceries while hungry or add small items to an online order. I try to set a budget and make a list before shopping, which helps me notice when something is an impulse purchase. It does not work every time, but waiting a day before buying a non-urgent item often shows whether I actually need it."],
  [/do people buy things they don.t need/i, "Yes, people sometimes buy things they do not need because of advertising, discounts or the excitement of something new. Online shopping makes it especially easy to add an item without thinking much about it. People may also buy for social reasons, such as keeping up with a trend. A budget and a short pause before checkout can help separate a real need from a passing want."],
  [/do you think it is the rich people.s responsibility to donate money to people in need/i, "People with significant wealth have more ability to support others, so donating can make a real difference. I would not say charity is their only responsibility; paying taxes fairly and treating employees well also matter. Donations are most useful when they support effective services and respect the needs of recipients. Society should not rely only on individual generosity to address problems that need public solutions."],
  [/do you think successful movies should have well-known actors or actresses in leading roles/i, "Well-known actors can attract an audience and help a film secure funding, but they do not guarantee a strong story or performance. New actors may fit a role better and bring a fresh energy. A successful film needs a combination of writing, direction and acting that works together. I am more likely to remember a convincing character than recognise every person in the cast."],
  [/do you think companies should donate money to help society/i, "Companies can support society by donating to local services, education or environmental projects, especially when the cause connects with the community they operate in. Donations should be transparent and not used to distract from poor working conditions or harmful products. A company also helps society by paying staff fairly and reducing waste. Financial support is valuable, but responsible daily behaviour matters just as much."],
  [/do you think customer satisfaction is important for a company/i, "Yes. Satisfied customers are more likely to return and recommend a business, which is important for long-term success. A company can learn from complaints and improve a product before a small problem becomes widespread. However, pleasing every customer is impossible, so firms need to balance feedback with safety, costs and fair treatment of staff. Trust grows when a company responds honestly and fixes mistakes."],
  [/should parents give children advice/i, "Parents should give advice when a child faces a choice or a risk they may not understand, but they should explain their reasoning and listen to the child's view. Advice is more useful when it helps a child compare options than when it simply gives an order. For everyday decisions, letting children try and learn from small mistakes can build independence and confidence."],
  [/what kind of advice should parents give to their children/i, "Parents should give practical, honest advice about safety, relationships and important choices, while recognising that their children may want a different future. For example, they can help a teenager compare college options without choosing a major for them. Good advice includes reasons and leaves room for questions. It should guide a child to think, rather than make them feel that only one path is acceptable."],
  [/should parents help their kids with their homework/i, "Parents can help by providing a quiet place, asking what the child has tried and explaining a difficult idea when needed. They should avoid completing the work for the child, because that hides what the student understands. If a problem remains unclear, it is better to contact the teacher. Support should make the child more independent over time, not create a habit of waiting for an answer."],
  [/what can children do to help their parents/i, "Children can help with age-appropriate chores, such as tidying their room, setting the table or looking after a pet with an adult's guidance. They can also show patience when parents are busy and share how they are feeling. The point is not to make a child responsible for adult problems. Small contributions help family members cooperate and teach children that a home is a shared responsibility."],
  [/what are the personalities of people whose job is to give advice to others/i, "People who give advice professionally need to listen carefully, ask clear questions and avoid judging someone too quickly. They should be calm and honest about what they know, while respecting privacy. A counsellor, for instance, cannot make a decision for a client but can help them see options. Empathy matters, but so does knowing when a problem needs a specialist or a different kind of support."],
  [/what are the problems if you ask too many people for advice/i, "Asking many people can expose someone to useful perspectives, but it can also create confusion because each person has different values and experience. They may delay a decision while trying to satisfy everyone. It helps to choose two or three people who understand the issue, compare their reasons and then decide based on one's own priorities. Advice should inform a choice, not transfer responsibility for it."],
  [/why do some people think it is better to ask for advice from friends than from parents/i, "Some people ask friends because they feel less pressure and expect them to understand their current life, especially with relationships or study. Parents may have more experience and know the person's history, but their advice can feel protective or old-fashioned. Neither source is always better. People should consider who understands the particular problem and be willing to hear a different point of view."],
  [/how can leaders encourage their employees|how can employers encourage their staff/i, "Leaders can encourage staff by setting clear goals, recognising useful work and giving people a real chance to develop. Flexible schedules or training may matter more than a one-off gift, depending on what employees need. Managers should also ask for feedback and act on reasonable concerns. Encouragement feels genuine when it is fair and consistent, rather than appearing only when a deadline is close."],
  [/when should parents encourage their children/i, "Parents should encourage children when they are trying something difficult, recovering from a setback or building a new habit. Specific encouragement, such as noticing that a child kept practising, is more useful than saying they are perfect. Children also need honest feedback, so praise should not hide a problem they can work on. Support helps most when it respects the child's effort and interests."],
  [/what kind of encouragement should parents give/i, "Parents should give encouragement that recognises effort, persistence and kindness rather than only high marks or winning. They can say what the child did well and suggest one next step, which makes praise feel believable. Constantly comparing a child with a sibling may create pressure. Encouragement should help children feel capable while making it clear that mistakes are a normal part of learning."],
  [/do you think it is good for children to make noise/i, "Some noise is a normal part of children playing and learning, and it can show that they feel comfortable expressing themselves. The important point is where and when it happens; loud games are not suitable in a library or late at night. Adults can set limits without expecting children to be silent all the time. Outdoor spaces give them room to be active while considering other people."],
  [/do you think children can learn about the law outside of school/i, "Yes. Children see rules in everyday life, such as road signs, public transport and rules for using a sports centre. Parents can explain why a rule exists when they encounter it, while news stories can provide age-appropriate examples. School gives a structured foundation, but daily experiences help children understand how rules protect people and what can happen when they are ignored."],
  [/do you think art classes are important for children/i, "Art classes are useful because they let children experiment with materials, express ideas and develop patience. They also give students who are less confident in academic subjects another way to feel capable. Not every child needs to become an artist, and classes should not focus only on producing neat work. The value is in practising observation and creativity in a supportive setting."],
  [/are there many people who can speak foreign languages in your country/i, "Many people in China study English at school, but the number who feel comfortable speaking it in daily life is smaller. Some people also learn Japanese, Korean or other languages for work and travel. Ability varies with access to practice and education. Online media and international workplaces create more chances to use a language, but regular conversation is still the best way to build confidence."],
  [/does speaking other languages help at work/i, "It can help people communicate with international clients, read material that is not translated and work with colleagues in other countries. The value depends on the industry; it may be essential in tourism or trade but less important in a local role. Language ability is not enough on its own, though. People also need professional skills and the ability to explain ideas clearly."],
  [/do children communicate more with teachers or with parents/i, "Most children probably talk more with their parents because they share a home and everyday routines. Teachers see them regularly in a different setting and may be easier to approach about schoolwork or a subject they enjoy. The balance changes with age and family circumstances. What matters is that children have at least one trusted adult who listens without immediately judging them."],
  [/is it easy to identify children.s talents/i, "Some talents are visible early, such as a strong interest in music or sport, but it can take time to recognise others. A quiet child may be good at careful observation or helping classmates, which is less obvious in a competition. Adults should offer children different activities and notice what they enjoy and practise. Early labels can be limiting, so opportunities matter more than deciding a talent too quickly."],
  [/do you think children should receive some musical education/i, "Yes, basic music education helps children listen carefully, notice rhythm and experience making something together. It does not need to lead to professional training; singing or trying a simple instrument can be enough. Some students may discover a lasting interest, while others gain confidence and enjoy the activity. Schools should make lessons welcoming rather than judge every child by performance."],
  [/do rewards help a child become better/i, "Rewards may encourage a child to practise, especially when a new task feels difficult, but they do not automatically build lasting motivation. A child who reads only to earn a prize may stop when the reward disappears. Specific praise and helping the child notice their progress can work alongside occasional rewards. The goal is for them to value the skill itself, not just what they receive."],
  [/do you think galleries and museums should be free of charge/i, "Public museums should ideally offer free entry to their main collections, so cost does not prevent families or students from learning. Special exhibitions may charge a modest fee because they can be expensive to organise. Museums still need funding for staff and conservation, so governments or sponsors can help cover the cost. Free access is valuable when it is paired with stable support for the institution."],
  [/should schools teach children about animals/i, "Yes, schools can teach children how to care for animals and behave safely around unfamiliar ones. A short lesson could explain that pets need food, exercise and regular attention, not just affection. This helps children understand responsibility before they ask for a pet. Lessons should use age-appropriate examples and respect animal welfare rather than encouraging children to handle wildlife."],
  [/should students learn to do group work/i, "Yes, students should practise group work because many jobs require people to share information and divide responsibilities. A school project gives them a low-risk chance to listen, explain an idea and resolve a disagreement. Group work should not replace individual tasks, though, because each student also needs to develop their own understanding. Clear roles and fair assessment help ensure that everyone contributes."],
  [/should children be taught to be kind to others/i, "Yes, children should learn kindness through what adults model and how they treat people around them. Simple habits, such as including a classmate or offering help when someone is upset, show that kindness is practical rather than abstract. Children also need to learn boundaries, so being kind does not mean agreeing to everything. Parents and teachers can discuss situations and let children practise responding."],
  [/should teachers know what children are doing all the time/i, "No, teachers cannot and should not monitor every moment. They need to supervise children for safety and notice when someone is struggling, but students also need privacy and chances to make small decisions independently. A teacher can set clear expectations and check in at suitable times. Trust and open communication are more helpful than constant observation, which could make children feel anxious."],
  [/should parents teach their children to spend money freely or to save money/i, "Parents should teach children to save some money while also allowing them to spend a reasonable amount on things they enjoy. A child can divide pocket money into savings and spending, then decide what to buy. That gives them practice comparing choices and understanding that money is limited. The goal is not to make children afraid to spend, but to help them make thoughtful decisions."],
  [/should schools teach children how to manage money from a young age/i, "Yes, schools can teach basic money skills such as budgeting, comparing prices and recognising online scams. These are useful whether a student later works, studies or manages a household. A classroom exercise could ask students to plan a small event within a fixed budget. Parents also influence habits, but schools can ensure every child learns the basics, even if money is rarely discussed at home."],
  [/should children learn to use ai/i, "Children should learn what AI can and cannot do before relying on it for schoolwork. They need to check facts, protect personal information and understand that a confident answer may still be wrong. A teacher can demonstrate how to compare an AI response with a reliable source. The aim is informed use, not teaching every child to build AI systems or encouraging them to avoid the technology entirely."],
  [/should students have physical education and do sports at school/i, "Yes. Physical education gives students regular movement, teaches basic skills and can help them manage stress after lessons. It also lets children try sports they may not have access to outside school. Not every student is competitive, so classes should include different activities and focus on participation as well as performance. Schools need safe facilities and enough choice for students with different abilities."],
  [/should parents reward children/i, "Parents can reward children when the reward recognises effort or a meaningful improvement, rather than every routine action. A small treat after a child has practised something consistently can encourage them, but praise and time together also matter. Parents should explain what they are recognising, so children understand the behaviour. Rewards work best as one form of encouragement, not the main reason to act well."],
  [/should teachers make learning in their classes fun/i, "Teachers should make lessons engaging, but fun should support learning rather than replace it. A short game or practical example can help students remember a difficult idea and take part more confidently. Some subjects still require careful practice, so not every class needs to feel like entertainment. A good teacher varies the approach and checks that students understand the material, not just that they enjoyed the activity."],
  [/why do some people like to watch sports events/i, "People watch sports because they enjoy the competition, the skill and the feeling of supporting a team. A close match can be exciting because the result may change in the final minutes. Watching with friends adds a social element, while a major event gives supporters something to share with a large crowd. Not everyone follows sport, but it can create a strong sense of belonging."],
  [/where do people normally watch sports events/i, "People watch sports at home, in a pub or cafe, or at the stadium, depending on the importance of the match and their budget. Home is comfortable and gives viewers replays, while a live venue offers atmosphere and a closer sense of the action. Friends may gather together for a final or a local game. The best place depends on whether someone values convenience or the crowd."],
  [/what are the advantages of watching sports events online/i, "Watching online is convenient because people can follow a match at home or on a phone, often with replays and several camera angles. It may also cost less than travelling to a stadium. The downside is that the screen cannot recreate the noise and shared excitement of a live crowd, and the stream may be delayed. Online viewing suits people who want flexibility or cannot attend in person."],
  [/why do some people spend a lot going to other countries to watch sports events/i, "Some supporters travel abroad because a major match or tournament is a rare experience, especially if their favourite team is playing. They may also want to meet other fans and see the host city while they are there. Tickets, travel and accommodation can be expensive, so it is usually a special trip rather than a regular habit. For committed supporters, sharing the atmosphere can justify the cost."],
  [/why do some children want to become doctors/i, "Children may want to become doctors because they admire someone who helped their family or because the job seems respected and useful. Television and books can also make medicine look exciting. As they learn more, some discover that the work involves long training and emotional pressure. It is a good ambition to explore, but children should be able to change their minds as their interests develop."],
  [/why is some doctors. pay high and others. low/i, "Doctors' pay varies with their speciality, experience, location and employer. A specialist who has completed many years of training may earn more than a new doctor, while public and private hospitals follow different pay systems. Demand and working hours also matter. Pay is one way to recognise expertise, but fair conditions and enough staff are important too, because long hours can affect both doctors and patients."],
  [/why are there special foods on special occasions or events/i, "Special foods help mark an occasion and bring people together around a shared custom. In China, families often eat dumplings during Lunar New Year because preparing and sharing them is part of the celebration. Some foods also carry symbolic meanings, such as wishing someone good luck. The recipe may change over time, but eating it together helps people feel connected to family and tradition."],
  [/what are the differences between everyday food and festival food|what are the differences between special food in china and other countries/i, "Everyday food is usually chosen for convenience, price and nutrition, while festival food may take longer to prepare and be shared in larger portions. In China, dumplings or mooncakes are linked to particular celebrations, whereas other countries may have their own seasonal dishes. The exact food differs, but the role is similar: it makes an occasion feel distinct and gives people a reason to gather."],
  [/why are there so few top athletes/i, "Very few people reach the top because it takes a combination of talent, years of structured training, good coaching and strong physical and mental health. Access to facilities and financial support also affects who can continue. Many talented athletes face injuries or other responsibilities before reaching elite level. Success is not only individual ability; it also depends on the system that supports people along the way."],
  [/what qualities should an athlete have/i, "An athlete needs discipline, resilience and the ability to learn from a coach, as well as physical ability in their sport. They have to practise consistently and recover properly after setbacks or injuries. In team sports, communication and cooperation matter too. Natural talent can help someone begin, but long-term progress depends on good habits and staying focused when results are disappointing."],
  [/why do many people like listening to music while doing sports/i, "Music can make exercise feel less repetitive and help people keep a steady pace, especially during a run or workout. Some choose energetic songs to improve their mood, while others listen to something calm when stretching. It is not suitable for every activity, because people need to hear instructions or traffic in some settings. The choice depends on safety and the kind of exercise."],
  [/are children unhappy with the school rules/i, "Some children dislike rules that feel strict or do not seem to have a clear reason, such as limits on phones or long homework. They are more likely to accept a rule when it protects safety or gives everyone a fair chance to learn. Schools should explain expectations and listen when a rule causes a problem. A rule can provide structure without treating students as if they have no judgement."],
  [/do you think students should learn to grow plant/i, "Yes, growing a plant can teach students patience and basic responsibility. They need to water it regularly and notice what changes, which is a practical way to learn about living things. A school does not need a large garden; pots on a windowsill can work. It should be an enjoyable activity, though, rather than another competition with marks attached."],
  [/can a child learn skills from another child/i, "Yes. Children often explain things in language their classmates understand, and they may feel less nervous asking a friend than an adult. A child who has learned to use a new game or craft can show another one step at a time. Adults still need to check safety and correct misunderstandings, but peer learning can make practice more relaxed and collaborative."],
  [/do you think teachers are able to spot the unhappy children/i, "A careful teacher can often notice changes, such as a child becoming unusually quiet, missing activities or struggling to focus. They may not know the reason immediately, so it helps to speak privately and avoid making assumptions. Some children hide how they feel, which is why a trusting classroom matters. Teachers can offer support and involve parents or counsellors when needed."],
  [/do you think learning biology is interesting for children/i, "Yes, biology can be interesting because children can connect it with animals, plants and their own bodies. A simple activity, such as watching a seed grow, makes an idea easier to remember than a list of terms. Some topics require careful explanation, but curiosity is a good starting point. Practical examples help children see why the subject matters in everyday life."],
  [/do people learn any languages other than english/i, "Yes. In China, some people study Japanese, Korean, French or another language for travel, work or personal interest. English is common in education, but it is not the only useful choice. A language becomes easier to maintain when learners have a reason to use it, such as talking to friends or following media. Access to classes and regular practice also makes a difference."],
  [/do you think people today should learn about ai technology/i, "Yes, people should understand the basics of AI so they can use it carefully and recognise its limits. Students and workers may encounter AI tools in ordinary tasks, from translation to data analysis. They do not all need to become programmers, but they should know that outputs can be inaccurate or biased. Basic digital judgement is now part of being an informed user."],
  [/do people like things of memorial significance/i, "Many people value objects that remind them of a person or an important time, such as a photograph, a ticket or a handwritten note. The object may have little resale value, but its story makes it meaningful. People differ in how much they keep; some prefer a few important things, while others save many mementos. The personal connection matters more than the object itself."],
  [/do you think there are too many subjects for students to learn/i, "Some students do feel overloaded when they have many subjects, frequent homework and little time to explore any topic deeply. A broad curriculum is useful because children may discover an interest they would not otherwise try. Schools need to balance that range with realistic workloads and time for practice. The problem is not simply the number of subjects, but how much work is expected in each one."],
  [/do you think children are born smart or they learn to become smart/i, "Children may start with different strengths, but experience, teaching and practice have a large effect on how those strengths develop. A child who asks questions and receives patient feedback can make progress in a subject that once seemed difficult. Calling someone naturally smart can overlook the effort behind their achievement. I think ability grows when children get support and opportunities to try."],
  [/do you think smart children are happier than other children/i, "Not necessarily. A child who learns quickly may enjoy school, but they can also feel pressure to keep getting high marks or struggle to connect with classmates. Happiness depends on relationships, confidence and feeling safe, not only on academic ability. Schools should give bright children suitable challenges while helping them develop interests and friendships outside their strongest subject."],
  [/is it important for schools to identify and develop each student.s talents/i, "Yes, schools should help students notice what they are good at, whether that is science, sport, art or working with other people. A talent can grow when a teacher gives useful feedback and a chance to practise. Identification should not become a fixed label, because children may develop new interests later. The aim is to open opportunities, not rank students too early."],
  [/do you think school rules are important/i, "Yes, clear school rules help protect students and give teachers a fair way to manage a shared classroom. Expectations about safety, attendance and respect make it easier for everyone to learn. Rules work better when students understand why they exist and when the same standard applies to everyone. Too many restrictions can feel controlling, so schools should focus on behaviour that affects others."],
  [/is it important for everyone to have a specialized skill/i, "It is useful to develop a skill in depth, but not everyone needs one narrow specialisation. People also benefit from communication, problem-solving and the ability to learn something new. A specialist can contribute strong expertise to a team, while broader skills help them adapt when work changes. The right balance depends on the person's goals and the kind of work they want to do."],
  [/do you think it is easier for children to learn new skills than for adults/i, "Children may have more time to practise and can be less worried about making mistakes, so some skills feel easier to learn young. Adults bring experience and discipline, though, and can understand why a skill is useful. I learned mathematics at university more effectively when I could connect new ideas with earlier knowledge. Age matters less than practice, motivation and the quality of guidance."],
  [/do children these days still like doing arts and crafts/i, "Yes, many children still enjoy drawing, building models or making things from paper, especially when the activity lets them choose what to create. Screens compete for their attention, but hands-on projects give an immediate result they can show someone. Schools and families can encourage it by providing simple materials and not judging every finished piece. Enjoyment matters more than producing perfect work."],
  [/do you think parents should be responsible for teaching children to save time/i, "Parents can help children learn to use time sensibly by showing them how to prepare for school, break a task into steps and leave time for rest. The goal should be independence, not filling every minute with planned activity. A child can practise packing a bag the night before, for example. Parents should guide them while allowing room for play and their own choices."],
  [/is it good to reward children too often/i, "Rewards can encourage a child when they recognise real effort, but giving one for every small action may make the child expect a prize before helping. Praise and a chance to feel capable are also powerful. I think rewards work best when they are occasional and proportionate, with the reason explained. Children should learn that responsibility and kindness matter even when no reward is offered."],
  [/do you think schools should teach children to do household chores/i, "Yes, simple chores can teach children to take responsibility for a shared home and look after themselves. Tasks such as clearing a table or putting away laundry are appropriate when matched to the child's age. It should not interfere with study or become unpaid work that replaces an adult's responsibilities. Doing chores together also gives families a practical way to share time and teach a routine."],
  [/do you prefer to visit well-developed cities or cities with a long history/i, "I would choose a city with a long history if I had time to explore, because older streets and buildings show how people's lives changed. A well-developed city may be more convenient for transport and services, so it can suit a shorter work trip. The two qualities can exist together. My choice depends on whether I want to learn about a place or simply move around efficiently."],
  [/is it necessary to keep learning after graduating from school/i, "Yes, because work, technology and people's interests continue to change after formal education ends. Learning does not always mean taking another degree; it can be a short course, reading or asking an experienced colleague for guidance. I expect to keep developing data and communication skills after graduation. Ongoing learning helps people adapt, but it should remain manageable alongside work and family life."],
  [/do you think it is good for older adults to continue learning/i, "Yes. Learning gives older adults a way to stay curious, meet people and keep using their skills. It could be a language class, a computer course or something enjoyable like local history. The purpose does not have to be getting a qualification or finding a job. Classes should be accessible and welcoming, so people can learn at a comfortable pace without feeling judged."],
  [/do you think language learning is important/i, "Language learning is important because it lets people communicate more directly and understand another culture beyond translated words. It can help with work or travel, but it is also satisfying to read or watch something in its original language. Not everyone needs to become fluent in several languages. Even a basic level can make everyday interactions easier and show respect for the people someone meets."],
  [/do you think people nowadays prefer attending big parties or small gatherings with close friends/i, "Many people prefer a small gathering because it is easier to talk with everyone and the atmosphere feels more personal. A large party can be exciting for a wedding or graduation, but guests may not know one another well. I would choose a smaller group for an ordinary celebration and a bigger event for a major milestone. The occasion and the guest list make the difference."],
  [/do you still keep in touch with your friends from childhood\? why or why not/i, "Yes, I still keep in touch with one or two friends from childhood, although we do not meet as often now. We share memories and understand parts of each other's background without needing to explain everything. Our lives have changed, so the friendship takes more effort to maintain, but a message or occasional meal is enough to keep the connection warm."],
  [/how often do you visit your relatives or friends/i, "I see close friends most weeks, usually after class or at the weekend, while I visit relatives less often because they live farther away. We tend to meet for a meal or a family occasion. During exam periods I may be busier, so we keep in touch by message and arrange a longer visit when my schedule is calmer."],
  [/when would parents feel proud of their children/i, "Parents may feel proud when children make a sustained effort, treat others well or handle a setback with more independence. Good grades can be one reason, but they are not the only measure of progress. A child who admits a mistake and tries to fix it may show maturity that a test score cannot capture. Pride is strongest when it recognises the person, not just the result."],
  [/on what occasions would adults be proud of themselves/i, "Adults may feel proud after completing a difficult project, learning a new skill or supporting someone through a hard period. The achievement does not have to be public; keeping a promise to yourself can matter just as much. For example, finishing a degree while balancing other responsibilities can represent years of steady effort. Pride is most meaningful when it reflects something the person values."],
  [/who should people ask for help, colleagues or family members/i, "It depends on the problem. Colleagues are often the right people for a work task because they understand the system and can offer practical advice. Family members may give more personal support or help someone think through a major life choice. People should consider who has relevant experience and can keep the matter private, rather than assuming one group is always more helpful."],
  [/what kind of jobs are the most popular in your country/i, "In China, many young people aim for jobs in technology, healthcare, education, finance or government because these fields can offer stable prospects and room to develop. Popular choices change with the economy, and people do not all value the same things. Some prefer a secure salary, while others look for flexibility or meaningful work. A job's reputation is only one part of whether it suits someone."],
  [/what things do families keep for a long time/i, "Families often keep photographs, jewellery, letters, recipe books or furniture that belonged to an older relative. These things may not be expensive, but they carry memories of people and occasions. My family has kept a handwritten recipe notebook because it reminds us of meals prepared together. Objects stay meaningful when someone explains their story and passes that story on to younger family members."],
  [/what.s the difference between things valued by people in the past and today/i, "People in the past often valued objects that were difficult to replace, such as handmade furniture, tools or family photographs. Today, digital devices and online files are part of daily life, and people may replace them more often as technology changes. The difference is partly practical: production and storage have changed. Emotional value still matters, though, which is why people keep both old objects and digital memories."],
  [/what kinds of things are kept in museums/i, "Museums keep objects that help explain a subject or a period, such as tools, clothing, artworks, documents and archaeological finds. Some also collect recordings and photographs, which show how people lived rather than only what leaders did. Curators have to protect fragile items and provide context so visitors understand why they matter. A small ordinary object can be as revealing as a famous artefact."],
  [/why do people visit museums/i, "People visit museums to learn about history, art or science in a way that feels more direct than reading a short summary. Seeing an object in person can make its size, material and age easier to understand. Museums also offer activities for families and school groups. A good exhibition tells a clear story and gives visitors enough context without overwhelming them with dates and labels."],
  [/what negative effects does technology have on people.s relationships/i, "Technology can interrupt face-to-face conversations when people keep checking their phones, and short messages can be misunderstood without tone or expression. Social media may also make someone compare their private life with carefully selected posts. Still, it helps distant friends stay connected. The effect depends on how it is used: setting a phone aside during important conversations can protect attention without giving up the benefits."],
  [/what makes a popular tv or online program/i, "A popular programme usually has a clear idea, engaging presenters and episodes that give viewers a reason to return. It might be funny, useful or connected to a topic people are already discussing. Good pacing matters, especially online, where viewers can leave quickly. Popularity also grows when people recommend a programme to friends, but a large audience does not necessarily mean it has lasting quality."],
  [/what kinds of tv or online programs are popular in your country/i, "In China, short videos, reality shows, dramas, comedy programmes and practical tutorials all attract large audiences. People choose different formats depending on how much time they have; a short clip fits a commute, while a drama is something to watch in the evening. Online services make it easy to follow a niche interest as well as mainstream entertainment. Trends change quickly as new platforms appear."],
  [/why aren.t young people willing to listen to the experiences of older people/i, "Some young people assume older people's experience belongs to a different time and will not help with problems they face now. They may also feel that advice is being imposed rather than offered. It helps when an older person listens first and connects their experience to the young person's situation. Then the conversation feels like an exchange, not a lecture."],
  [/why do some young people feel bored when talking with old people/i, "Young people may feel bored if the conversation centres on topics they do not know or if they are expected only to listen. A difference in pace and communication habits can also make it harder to connect. Shared activities, such as cooking or looking through family photographs, give both generations something concrete to discuss. Boredom often reflects the way people interact, not a lack of anything to learn."],
  [/what are the differences between young people.s leisure time activities and those of old people/i, "Young people often spend leisure time with friends, online media or organised activities, while older adults may prefer walking, family visits or hobbies they have kept for years. These are broad patterns rather than rules; health, income and personality matter too. Technology has also made it easier for older people to watch programmes or contact friends online. People of every age need both social time and rest."],
  [/what kinds of things do the elderly remember for a long time/i, "Older people often remember events connected with strong emotions, such as a family milestone, a difficult period or a place where they grew up. Repeated routines and stories can also stay vivid for decades. A familiar song or smell may bring those memories back unexpectedly. I think people remember not only what happened but who was there and how that experience changed their lives."],
  [/what sports are popular among older people/i, "Many older adults enjoy walking, swimming, tai chi or badminton because these activities can be adjusted to different fitness levels. Some also play table tennis or join a local exercise group for the social side. The best choice depends on health and access to facilities. Regular, moderate activity is usually easier to maintain than a demanding sport that risks injury."],
  [/what do old people usually do in their daily life/i, "Older people's routines vary, but many spend time on household tasks, walking, seeing relatives, looking after grandchildren or meeting friends. Those who have retired may have more flexibility, while others still work or provide care. I would not assume that an older person is inactive. A regular routine can give structure, but having choice and social contact is important too."],
  [/why is there a generation gap between the young and the old/i, "A generation gap often comes from growing up with different technology, job markets and social expectations. Older people may value a stable career because that was important in their experience, while younger adults see changing jobs as normal. Family roles and media habits can also differ. The gap becomes smaller when people ask why a view developed instead of assuming the other generation is simply wrong."],
  [/has old people.s life quality improved when compared to the past/i, "In many ways, yes: older people today often have better healthcare, safer homes and more ways to stay in touch than previous generations. However, the benefits are not shared equally, and loneliness or the cost of care can still be serious problems. Life quality depends on health, income and social support, not only on technology. Improvements matter most when older adults can remain independent and connected."],
  [/what do older people and young people have in common|how can young people learn from older people/i, "Young and older people both want to feel respected, useful and connected to others, even if they express those needs differently. Younger people can learn practical judgement from an older person's experience, while older adults can learn about new technology or current pressures from the young. Sharing a task or family story makes the exchange natural. It works best when both generations are treated as people with something to contribute."],
  [/how can companies help protect the environment/i, "Companies can reduce waste in production, use energy more efficiently and design products that last longer or can be repaired. They can also be transparent about the materials and emissions in their supply chains. A business may find these changes costly at first, but saving energy and avoiding waste can reduce expenses over time. Government standards help ensure that responsible companies are not undercut by competitors doing less."],
  [/some people think pets should not be kept in cities|what are the advantages of keeping a pet/i, "Pets can give people companionship and a daily routine, and they may encourage owners to spend more time outside. The concern in cities is whether an animal has enough room, exercise and proper care in an apartment. I do not think pets should be banned, but owners need to choose an animal that fits their home and schedule. Responsible care matters more than the address."],
  [/why do people always tell children stories with animals/i, "Stories with animals let children explore human feelings and choices in a playful way. A fox, bear or small bird can represent curiosity, kindness or greed without making the lesson sound like a lecture. Children also enjoy imagining how an animal would speak and behave. The story can be entertaining first, while giving parents an easy way to talk about friendship or responsibility afterwards."],
  [/what are the advantages of growing vegetables or flowers at home|why do some people like to grow plants|why do some people prefer to grow their own fruits and vegetables instead of buying them from the market/i, "Growing plants gives people a relaxing activity and a visible result they can care for over time. Vegetables grown at home may feel fresher, and people know how they were treated, though the amount is usually small. Flowers can make a balcony or garden more pleasant. Not everyone has space or time, so it is a rewarding hobby rather than a replacement for buying food."],
  [/which is more important, protecting the environment or developing the economy/i, "The two goals should be planned together because economic growth depends on clean water, healthy people and reliable natural resources. A factory can create jobs, but pollution may leave nearby residents with health costs. Governments can encourage cleaner production and help workers move into new roles. Some trade-offs are unavoidable, but treating environmental protection as a later problem often makes it more expensive to solve."],
  [/what are the benefits of travelling abroad|who prefers travelling abroad\? the young or the old/i, "Travelling abroad exposes people to different customs, food and ways of organising daily life, which can make them more open-minded. Young adults may have more energy and flexibility, while older travellers may have more savings and time. Those are only general tendencies; health and personal interest matter more. Travel is most valuable when people engage with local life instead of only visiting familiar tourist sites."],
  [/how much time do you think people should spend on a trip abroad|what is the ideal length for a holiday/i, "There is no ideal length for everyone. A long weekend may be enough for a nearby city, while a distant destination needs more time to make the travel worthwhile. People should also consider their budget, work commitments and whether they want a relaxed visit or a full itinerary. I prefer leaving a little unplanned time, because rushing through every attraction can make a holiday feel like work."],
  [/where do people in your country often go for holidays|where do young people in your country often go for holidays/i, "People in China often visit nearby cities, coastal areas, mountain scenery or famous historic sites during holidays. Young travellers may choose places with good transport, affordable food and activities they can share with friends. Families often look for a mix of attractions and convenient accommodation. The choice depends on the season and budget, and popular destinations can become very crowded during national holidays."],
  [/how do people usually plan holidays/i, "People usually choose a destination first, then compare transport, accommodation and the time they can take off work or study. Many check reviews and weather before booking. Some prefer a detailed itinerary, while others reserve only the important parts and decide the rest after arriving. I think a plan should cover practical needs but leave room to change direction if a place is busy or the weather turns."],
  [/why do many countries try to attract people to visit/i, "Countries promote tourism because visitors spend money on accommodation, food, transport and local attractions, creating jobs and tax income. Tourism can also introduce a country's culture to a wider audience. The challenge is that popular sites may become crowded and local housing or services more expensive. Governments need to manage visitor numbers and direct some income back into the communities that receive tourists."],
  [/which form of vehicle is more popular in your country/i, "Motorcycles are probably the most common private vehicle in many Chinese cities because they are relatively affordable and easy to park, although electric bikes are especially widespread for short trips. Cars are more common among families who travel longer distances. The balance varies by region and income. Good public transport also affects what people choose, so popularity is not only about personal preference."],
  [/why do some people prefer to work by themselves/i, "Some people prefer working alone because they can concentrate, make decisions quickly and organise the task in their own way. This can suit writing or detailed analysis, where interruptions are costly. However, working alone means they have fewer chances to check an idea with someone else. I prefer to start a mathematics problem independently, then ask a classmate to review my reasoning if I reach a dead end."],
  [/what should a leader do to make team members want to follow him or her/i, "A leader should set a clear direction, listen to the team and take responsibility when something goes wrong. People are more willing to follow someone who explains decisions and treats different members fairly. In a student project, a leader can ask what each person is good at before dividing the work. Confidence helps, but trust comes from being consistent and giving the team credit."],
  [/how can you tell if a person is a good leader/i, "A good leader helps a group make progress without taking every decision for themselves. They explain the goal, listen when a plan is not working and make sure quieter members can contribute. For example, during a group assignment, a strong leader checks whether everyone understands their role instead of only chasing the deadline. The result matters, but so does how the team worked together."],
  [/why do some people start their own business/i, "People start a business because they see a need, want more independence or hope to earn a living from an idea they care about. Someone may notice that a neighbourhood lacks a quiet cafe, for example, and decide to serve local students. The freedom is attractive, but owners also take financial risks and work long hours. A good idea needs careful planning and customers."],
  [/what makes a business successful/i, "A business succeeds when it understands what customers need and provides that service reliably at a price they can accept. Good staff and careful cost management matter just as much as an original idea. A small cafe, for instance, can build regular customers by keeping the food consistent and listening to feedback. Success usually comes from improving over time, not from one clever advertisement."],
  [/what makes a business fail|is it easy to start up a company|is it difficult to do business in today.s world/i, "A business can fail when it misjudges demand, spends too much before earning steady income or ignores what customers say. Competition and rent can be difficult, especially for a new shop. Starting is easier than keeping the business healthy over several years. Owners need to understand their costs and be ready to change the product or service when the original plan is not working."],
  [/how do you balance life and work/i, "I try to protect time for sleep, exercise and people close to me instead of filling every free hour with work. During a busy week, I set a clear stopping time and choose the most important tasks first. That is not always possible near a deadline, but I can recover afterwards. A realistic routine is more sustainable than expecting perfect balance every day."],
  [/what are the benefits of working for an international company|what personal skills are required to work in an international company/i, "An international company can expose employees to different markets, working styles and colleagues from other countries. That can help them improve communication and see how decisions affect people in different places. Employees need clear communication, adaptability and respect for cultural differences. The experience can be valuable, although it may also involve time-zone meetings and faster changes than some people prefer."],
  [/what are the advantages and disadvantages of shopping online|what are the differences between online shopping and in-store shopping/i, "Online shopping offers a wider choice and saves travel time, and it is easy to compare prices. The disadvantage is that buyers cannot check the size or quality in person and may have to wait for delivery or return an item. In a shop, people can ask questions and take something home immediately. I use both, depending on whether trying the product matters."],
  [/why do some people not like shopping in small shops|what are the advantages and disadvantages of shopping in small shops/i, "Small shops can offer personal service and products chosen for the local community, but their prices may be higher and the selection more limited than a large chain. Some people also prefer one-stop shopping, which a small store cannot provide. Others value being recognised by the owner and supporting a neighbourhood business. Whether it feels convenient depends on what someone needs that day."],
  [/why are shopping malls so popular in china/i, "Shopping malls are popular because they combine shops, restaurants, cinemas and indoor places to spend time, which is convenient in hot, cold or rainy weather. Families can make a whole outing of it rather than shop for one item. Many malls are also close to public transport. They can be crowded and encourage extra spending, but their range of activities keeps people coming back."],
  [/what kind of things do people usually save money for/i, "People often save for housing, education, travel, a car or an expensive device, depending on their age and income. Some also keep an emergency fund for medical costs or a period without work. A clear goal makes it easier to put aside a small amount regularly. Saving is useful, but people also need to decide how much they can set aside without making daily life unnecessarily restrictive."],
  [/what little decisions do people make every day/i, "People make small decisions about what to eat, how to travel, what to wear and how to organise their time. Each choice seems minor, but together they shape daily routines and spending. I decide which assignment to work on first by checking its deadline and how much concentration it needs. A simple habit can save time, so not every small decision needs lengthy analysis."],
  [/who do young people like to share opinions with/i, "Young people often share opinions with close friends because they expect an honest response and have similar experiences. They may also talk to a parent or teacher when the issue involves a big decision and they want more perspective. Online groups make it easy to find people with the same interests, but not every reply is reliable. The best person to ask depends on the subject and the trust involved."],
  [/what kind of plans do people often make/i, "People commonly plan study or work tasks, trips, family events and how to manage their money. Some plans cover only the next day, while others involve a career or a move to another city. Planning helps people see what needs to happen first, but it should leave room for changes. I find a weekly study plan useful as long as I can adjust it when new work appears."],
  [/what important decisions do teenagers need to make after graduation/i, "After graduation, teenagers may choose a university, vocational training, a first job or a gap period. The decision matters, but it does not determine their entire life because people can change direction later. They should compare the cost, their interests and the opportunities available, then talk to someone with relevant experience. Parents can advise them, but the young person should have a real voice in the choice."],
  [/who can children turn to for help when making a decision/i, "Children can ask parents or teachers because those adults know them and can explain possible consequences. For a school subject or activity, a teacher may understand the options better; for a family matter, a parent may have more context. A trusted older sibling can also be approachable. Adults should help children think through a choice rather than immediately make it for them."],
  [/how do people usually make important decisions/i, "People usually gather information, compare likely outcomes and think about what matters most to them. For a university choice, someone might compare the course, cost and distance from home, then ask current students about their experience. There is rarely a perfect option, so setting a deadline can prevent endless delay. Advice helps, but the person who will live with the result should make the final decision."],
  [/what goals should a society have|what goals do people at your age have/i, "A society should aim for basic security, good education, accessible healthcare and fair opportunities, while also protecting the environment for the future. People in their early twenties often focus on finishing education, finding stable work and becoming financially independent. Those goals depend on one another: it is difficult to plan a career if housing or health is insecure. Progress should be measured by people's quality of life."],
  [/what problems does it cause for transportation when everyone commutes at the same time/i, "When most people travel at the same time, roads and public transport become crowded, so journeys take longer and buses can be delayed. The pressure is concentrated around office areas and major stations during rush hour. Flexible work hours can spread demand, while reliable buses and trains give commuters an alternative to driving. Building more roads alone often attracts more traffic, so the timing and travel choices matter too."],
  [/what are the differences between those tall buildings in your country/i, "Tall buildings in China differ in what they are used for and how they connect with the street. Some are mainly apartments, while others combine offices, shops and public facilities. Newer developments may have underground parking and shared amenities, whereas older residential towers can have smaller lifts and fewer green spaces. Their design also depends on location, land costs and local planning rules."],
  [/what are the advantages of living in tall buildings/i, "Tall buildings allow many households to live near workplaces, transport and shops, which can reduce long commutes. Residents may also have shared facilities such as a gym or a reception desk. The trade-off is dependence on lifts and less private outdoor space. They work well when the surrounding area has good services and the building is maintained, rather than simply being tall."],
  [/what kind of interior design style do most people like/i, "Many people prefer a simple, modern interior with practical storage, neutral colours and enough natural light. That style is easy to maintain and does not feel crowded in a smaller apartment. Preferences still vary: some people like traditional wooden details or brighter colours that show their personality. In the end, a comfortable layout matters more than copying a style that looks good in photographs."],
  [/how would people usually respond to noises in your country|what kinds of noises are there in our life/i, "People respond differently depending on the source and time of day. They may accept the sound of children playing during the afternoon but complain about building work or loud music late at night. Common urban noises include traffic, construction, public announcements and neighbours. Better insulation and clear quiet hours can help, but people also need to consider how much their own noise affects others."],
  [/which area is exposed to noise more, the city or the countryside/i, "Cities are generally noisier because traffic, construction, shops and large numbers of people are close together. The countryside is quieter overall, although farms, highways or seasonal machinery can still create noise. The difference is also about distance: rural residents may have fewer sources nearby, while city residents hear several at once. Good planning can protect quiet areas even within a city."],
  [/why do people go to crowded places/i, "People go to crowded places because popular shops, transport hubs and events offer something they cannot easily find elsewhere. A concert, market or festival can feel exciting precisely because many people are sharing the same experience. At other times, people have little choice, such as commuting through a busy station. The attraction depends on the purpose; crowds are enjoyable for an event but tiring during an everyday errand."],
  [/which cities are overcrowded in your country|what kinds of facilities do big cities have/i, "China's largest cities, including Beijing, Shanghai and Shenzhen, attract people for jobs, universities, hospitals and transport links. Those services create opportunity, but housing costs and crowded commutes can be difficult. Smaller cities may offer a calmer pace, though there are fewer specialised jobs. Whether a big city is a good choice depends on someone's career, family needs and ability to manage the cost of living."],
  [/what are the disadvantages of living in a very famous city/i, "A famous city can bring high housing costs, crowded public spaces and a large number of tourists, which may make ordinary errands less convenient for residents. Popular areas can also lose some local character when businesses mainly serve visitors. At the same time, residents benefit from jobs and cultural events. Tourism is easier to live with when visitor numbers are managed and neighbourhood needs are considered."],
  [/for those who live in cities, is it because they want to or have to/i, "It is usually a mixture. Some people choose a city because it offers work, education, cultural events and services they enjoy. Others move because their field has few jobs elsewhere or their family needs access to a hospital or university. People may want the opportunities but not the expense or pace. Their reasons can change as work and family circumstances change."],
  [/what advantages can tourism bring to a city|what are the benefits of urbanization/i, "Tourism brings spending to hotels, restaurants, transport and cultural sites, and it can support jobs that might otherwise be limited. Urban growth can also make public services more efficient when many people live close together. The risk is pressure on housing, transport and local identity. A city benefits most when it invests visitor income in public spaces and ensures residents can still afford to live there."],
  [/what types of buildings are popular in your country/i, "Apartment blocks are common in Chinese cities because they make efficient use of limited urban land. People also value shopping centres, public libraries and transport hubs that combine several services in one place. In smaller towns, low-rise housing is more common. What becomes popular depends on the cost of land and how people travel; a useful building needs to fit its neighbourhood, not just look impressive."],
  [/how can we solve the traffic jam problem|what are good ways to manage traffic/i, "Cities can reduce traffic jams by making buses and trains frequent, connecting them well and providing safe routes for walking and cycling. Staggered work hours can also spread the morning rush. Wider roads may help at one junction, but they can encourage more people to drive later. Good traffic management combines public transport, sensible parking rules and planning that puts daily services closer to where people live."],
  [/what are the popular water sports in your country/i, "Swimming is the most accessible water activity, while boating, kayaking and paddleboarding are popular in places with lakes or a safe coastline. Competitive sports such as rowing also have a following, especially at universities. Access depends on facilities and safety, so not every region offers the same choices. People are more likely to try a sport when lessons and equipment are affordable."],
  [/what do you think of communicating via social media|what are the differences between making friends in real life and online|what are the differences between online and face-to-face communication|what are the differences between online and face-to-face communication/i, "Social media makes it easy to keep in touch across distance and share small updates, but written messages can hide tone and be misunderstood. Meeting in person gives people more cues, such as expression and timing, which helps build trust. Online conversations can still develop into real friendships when people are honest and make time to talk. I see the two forms as useful in different situations."],
  [/which is more effective, online advertising or offline advertising|what are the advantages and disadvantages of advertisements|why are many advertisements endorsed by celebrities|what is the most important factor in an advertisement|why are some advertisements boring/i, "Online advertising can reach a specific audience and show whether people clicked, while television or outdoor adverts may reach people who are not actively searching. A celebrity can attract attention, but it does not prove that a product works. The most effective advert is clear about what it offers and who it suits. Repetition or exaggerated claims can annoy people and damage trust."],
  [/how does technology affect the law/i, "Technology creates new situations that existing laws may not cover, such as how companies collect personal data or how online fraud is investigated. It can also help authorities organise evidence and make public services easier to access. Laws need to keep pace without restricting useful tools unnecessarily. The difficult part is protecting people's rights while allowing innovation, so updates should be based on real problems and explained clearly."],
  [/what kind of videos do people in your country like to watch|which is more helpful, watching videos or reading books|what makes a video go viral online/i, "People in China watch short comedy clips, food and travel videos, tutorials and livestreams. A video spreads quickly when it is easy to understand, emotionally engaging or useful enough for viewers to share. Video works well for demonstrations, while books usually give more depth and make it easier to review details. The better format depends on whether someone wants a quick example or a fuller explanation."],
  [/how does technology help people make plans/i, "Calendar apps and shared documents help people keep deadlines, appointments and group tasks in one place. A reminder is useful when several classmates need to meet around different schedules. Technology also makes it easy to change a plan and tell everyone quickly. It cannot decide what matters most, though, so people still need to review priorities rather than follow every notification automatically."],
  [/what are the advantages and disadvantages of ai|how can ai help in our lives/i, "AI can save time on routine tasks, summarise information and help people explore ideas, which is useful in study and some jobs. However, it can produce confident mistakes, reflect bias in its data or encourage people to stop checking sources. I would use it as a starting point and verify important claims. Its value depends on whether people understand both its limits and its purpose."],
  [/what are the differences between old and young people when using apps|why do some people not like using apps/i, "Younger people often learn new apps quickly and use them for messaging, entertainment and payments. Older adults may prefer a smaller number of familiar tools, especially if menus change often or text is hard to read. Some people of any age dislike apps because of privacy concerns or too many notifications. Good design should be clear and give users control, rather than assuming everyone wants more features."],
  [/what.s the influence of technology on museums|what are the benefits of technology for learning history/i, "Technology lets museums show maps, recordings and interactive displays that help visitors picture how people lived in the past. It can also make collections available online to people who cannot travel. A digital display should support the real objects and historical context, not replace them with entertainment alone. Visitors learn most when the technology answers a question and is accessible to different ages."],
  [/what are the differences between the technology of the past and that of today|what technology do young people like to use/i, "Technology today is smaller, faster and connected to the internet, so one phone can replace several older devices. Young people use phones, laptops and wireless headphones for study, socialising and entertainment. Earlier technology was often limited to one task but could be easier to repair. The change has made information more accessible, though it has also increased distractions and the amount of personal data people share."],
  [/how does the internet benefit people/i, "The internet gives people quick access to information, public services, learning materials and communication across long distances. Someone can attend an online class or arrange an appointment without travelling across town. It also helps small businesses reach customers. The benefit depends on access and the ability to judge information, because unreliable sources and scams can make the same convenience harmful."],
  [/what do you think of people using their mobile phones during a meal/i, "A quick check for an urgent message is understandable, but scrolling through a meal can make companions feel ignored. Meals are one of the few times families or friends can talk without a formal plan, so attention matters. I think people should agree on a simple expectation, such as putting phones away unless someone is waiting for important news. It is about courtesy, not banning devices completely."],
  [/how does technology affect the way people spend their leisure time/i, "Technology gives people more ways to relax, from streaming films and playing games to joining an online class. It can also make hobbies easier to share with friends. The downside is that passive scrolling can take up more time than intended and crowd out sleep or exercise. I think it works best when people choose an activity deliberately instead of opening an app whenever they have a free moment."],
  [/technology has made it unnecessary to learn languages/i, "Translation tools are useful for signs, basic messages and unfamiliar words, but they cannot always capture tone, humour or cultural meaning. Learning a language also lets people build relationships without depending on a device. I would use technology to support practice, for example by listening to a podcast, but I do not think it makes language learning unnecessary. It changes how we learn, not why communication matters."],
  [/what kinds of movies are the most popular in china/i, "Commercial comedies, action films and large-scale historical stories tend to attract wide audiences in China. They are easy to watch with friends or family and often have well-known actors or strong visual effects. Smaller films can also become popular through word of mouth when the story feels fresh. I think people choose a film for the experience it offers, not only its genre."],
  [/why do people want to be popular|why are some students popular in school/i, "People often want to be popular because being accepted makes social life feel easier and gives them a sense of belonging. At school, students who are friendly, reliable or good at an activity may naturally attract others. Popularity can also come from confidence, although it does not always mean someone has close friendships. I think a few trusting relationships matter more than being known by everyone."],
  [/why do people need friends|what qualities make true friends/i, "Friends give people emotional support, shared experiences and someone to talk to outside their family. A true friend does not have to agree with every choice, but they should listen honestly and be dependable when things are difficult. For example, a classmate who checks in before an exam can ease stress without solving the problem for you. Trust grows through that kind of consistent attention."],
  [/what can we do to bring family members closer together/i, "Families can grow closer by making time for ordinary conversation and sharing responsibilities, rather than meeting only for formal occasions. A regular meal gives relatives a chance to catch up, while helping one another with practical tasks builds trust. People will not agree on everything, so listening matters as much as being together. A warm relationship depends on respect, not simply living under one roof."],
  [/how do people get along in a big family/i, "People in a big family get along better when they communicate clearly, respect personal boundaries and share responsibilities fairly. Regular meals can help relatives stay connected, but disagreements are easier to manage when people listen instead of keeping score. It also helps to avoid comparing siblings or cousins. Family members do not need identical opinions; mutual respect makes it possible to live with those differences."],
  [/how do people make friends in china|on what occasions do people like to make friends/i, "People often make friends through school, work, shared hobbies or introductions from someone they already know. In China, classmates may become close through group assignments or meals after class, because they see one another regularly. Meeting at an event can start a conversation, but repeated contact is what usually builds trust. A shared interest gives people an easy first topic without forcing instant closeness."],
  [/what sports games are popular in your country|what is the most popular sport in your country/i, "Football and basketball attract large audiences, and badminton is also widely played because it is easy to organise in schools and community gyms. Major international tournaments bring people together to watch, while local matches are more accessible for families. Popularity depends partly on facilities and media coverage. A sport that people can play near home is more likely to become part of ordinary life."],
  [/what apps are popular in your country/i, "Messaging and short-video apps are widely used in China, along with shopping, payment and map services. People rely on them for everyday tasks because several useful functions can sit in one place. Their popularity also reflects habit: friends and businesses are already using the same platforms. Convenience is a major reason, although people still need to manage screen time and protect personal information."],
  [/what kinds of books are popular in china/i, "Readers in China enjoy a broad range, including novels, practical nonfiction, history and online fiction. The popular choice depends on age and free time; some people read short stories on their phones during a commute, while others prefer a printed book at home. Online platforms make new writers easier to discover. A book becomes widely read when its subject connects with people's interests or daily concerns."],
  [/what are the advantages and disadvantages of being a famous child|what can children do with their fame/i, "Being famous as a child can bring opportunities, such as meeting interesting people or earning money, but it also puts private life under public attention before the child is mature enough to manage it. Adults may benefit financially from the child's image, so protection and informed consent matter. Fame should never take priority over education, friendships and the child's right to change their mind later."],
  [/when do people like to hold a party|what special events are celebrated in your country|what festivals are the most important for people in your country/i, "People usually hold parties for birthdays, weddings, graduations and traditional festivals such as Lunar New Year. These occasions give relatives or friends a reason to meet, share food and mark a change in someone's life. The scale can vary from a meal at home to a large gathering. For me, the conversation and sense of being together matter more than expensive decorations."],
  [/some people don.t mind others walking into their shot|when do people like taking photos/i, "People tend to take photos during holidays, family events, meals with friends and moments they find visually striking. If someone walks into the frame, some people do not mind because it makes the picture feel spontaneous; others prefer a clean composition and may wait for the space to clear. It is courteous to notice what someone is photographing and avoid blocking them when possible."],
  [/what kind of gifts do people usually bring when they visit others/i, "People often bring fruit, snacks, flowers or a small item for the household when visiting someone. The choice depends on the relationship and occasion; close friends may bring something casual, while a formal visit calls for a more considered gift. In China, people sometimes take fruit or tea to a relative's home. The gesture matters more than the price, as long as it suits the host."],
  [/what do people usually consider when buying gifts|which do people give on traditional festivals, red envelopes or other kinds of gifts|when do people normally send gifts to others/i, "People usually consider the recipient's interests, the occasion and their budget. A practical gift can be thoughtful if it shows that the giver has paid attention, while a red envelope is common for some Chinese festivals because the recipient can choose what they need. Gifts are also given for birthdays, weddings and graduations. The best choice is personal without making the other person feel obliged."],
  [/besides big historical events, what else is important when learning history/i, "Everyday life, local customs and ordinary people's experiences are important alongside wars and political leaders. They show how society actually worked: how families earned a living, what children learned and how people travelled. Family stories and old photographs can make a period easier to picture. Studying both daily life and major events gives a fuller explanation of why societies changed."],
  [/is it important to learn how people in the past lived their lives/i, "Yes, because knowing how people lived makes historical events less abstract. Their work, homes, food and family routines help explain what choices were available to them and why change mattered. For example, learning how people travelled can clarify why towns developed around rivers or roads. It should sit alongside political history, since ordinary experience and major events influence each other."],
  [/how do children learn history before they attend school/i, "Before school, children can learn history informally through family stories, old photographs, festivals and visits to local places. A grandparent describing how a neighbourhood used to look gives them a simple sense of the past. They do not need dates or formal terms yet; parents can answer questions and compare old and new objects. The aim is to make them curious about how life changes."],
  [/at what age do you think people should begin to learn history/i, "Around six or seven is a reasonable age to begin, when children can follow a simple story and understand the idea of 'long ago'. They can start with family history or familiar local places rather than memorising dates and dynasties. More formal timelines can come later, as their reading develops. Early lessons should build curiosity and a sense of change, not test how many facts they remember."],
  [/what should people wear to a job interview|why do we need to attend interviews when applying for jobs|what should people do to prepare for a job interview|why do some people dislike being interviewed|what kinds of people are usually interviewed/i, "An interview lets an employer understand how a candidate communicates and gives the candidate a chance to ask about the role. People should read about the organisation, prepare examples of their work and dress neatly in a way that suits the workplace. Some applicants dislike interviews because they feel nervous or are not used to selling their strengths. Clear questions and a respectful atmosphere help both sides."],
  [/why do some people prefer watching movies to reading books/i, "Movies are easier for some people because images, music and acting tell the story without requiring long periods of reading. They can also be watched together, which makes them a social activity. Books give readers more detail and room to imagine characters for themselves, though they take more time. I do not think one is better; people choose the format that fits their mood and attention."],
  [/what can we learn from history|why do people visit historical sites|why is it hard to protect historic buildings/i, "History helps people understand why a place or institution developed as it did, while historic sites make that past visible. Protecting old buildings can be expensive because they need specialist repairs and must still meet modern safety needs. Cities also face pressure to build housing and transport. I think preservation works best when an old site has a useful role, so local people have a reason to care for it."],
  [/what kind of music events do people like today|what kind of music events are there in your country|differences between old and young people.s music preferences|differences between listening to music at home and at a live concert/i, "People attend pop concerts, music festivals, classical performances and smaller local shows, depending on their taste and budget. Younger listeners may follow current artists, while older people often return to music they grew up with, although there is plenty of overlap. A concert offers atmosphere and a shared crowd; listening at home is cheaper and more comfortable. The choice depends on whether people want the performance or convenience."],
  [/what are the differences between reading a book and visiting a museum/i, "A book lets someone explore a subject at their own pace and return to details later. A museum offers objects, images and displays that make history or science feel more tangible. For example, reading about an old tool is different from seeing its size and wear in person. Books are easier to access, while museums can make a strong impression. The two forms often complement each other."],
  [/how can the arts spread positive emotions in society/i, "The arts give people a way to express feelings and share experiences that may be difficult to explain directly. A public concert or community mural can create a sense of connection among people who would not otherwise meet. Art can also help individuals process sadness or stress. It does not make every problem disappear, but it can make people feel understood and create space for a wider conversation."],
  [/what are the differences between the way adults learn and the way children learn|why is it easier for children to learn new things than for adults/i, "Children often learn through play and repetition, while adults tend to connect a new skill with a clear goal or previous experience. An adult learning software for work, for example, may practise only the functions needed for a task. Children can be less self-conscious, but adults are often more disciplined. The best method depends on the learner and the skill."],
  [/what are the differences between learning in a group and learning by yourself|differences between learning from a teacher and learning by oneself/i, "Learning alone lets people work at their own pace and focus on the parts they find difficult. A group offers immediate questions and different explanations, which can reveal a gap in someone's understanding. I often study mathematics on my own first, then discuss a proof with classmates if I am stuck. A mix is useful, because neither method suits every task."],
  [/what skills should children learn before they go to school/i, "Before school, children benefit from basic communication, listening, sharing and looking after simple needs, such as putting away their things. They do not need formal lessons in every subject. Being able to ask for help or take turns makes the first classroom experience easier. Parents can build these skills through play and daily routines rather than treating early childhood like an exam."],
  [/what would make a student happy at school/i, "Students are more likely to feel happy when they feel safe, have a few trusted friends and can see that they are making progress. A teacher who notices effort, not only top marks, can help a struggling student feel included. School should still be challenging, but students need time for interests and rest as well. Feeling supported makes it easier to learn."],
  [/what rules should students follow at school/i, "Students should follow rules that protect safety and let other people learn, such as arriving on time, listening when someone else is speaking and using phones only when a lesson allows it. Rules should be clear and applied consistently. A punishment alone may stop behaviour briefly, but explaining the reason helps students make a sensible choice even when a teacher is not watching."],
  [/how can parents teach children to obey rules/i, "Parents can explain the reason for a rule, agree on a clear consequence and follow the same standard themselves. If a child understands that a road rule protects people from injury, it feels less arbitrary than simply hearing 'because I said so'. It also helps to notice when a child makes the right choice. Consistency builds understanding better than shouting or changing expectations."],
  [/how do adults learn a new skill/i, "Adults often learn a new skill by setting aside regular time and practising the part they need in daily life. Someone learning a spreadsheet for work, for instance, can follow a short lesson and then use the tool on a real task. Feedback helps them catch mistakes early. Adults may have less free time than children, so a practical goal keeps the effort manageable."],
  [/how can parents and teachers teach children new skills|where can children learn new skills/i, "Children can learn skills at home, in class, through clubs or from other children. Parents and teachers help most by showing the steps, letting the child try and giving patient feedback instead of taking over. For example, a child learning to cook can begin with a simple safe task. Repeated practice in a supportive setting builds confidence as well as ability."],
  [/why should children learn new skills/i, "Learning new skills helps children become more independent and discover what they enjoy. A child who learns to prepare a simple meal, for example, gains a practical ability and confidence that they can improve with practice. It is also healthy for them to try things they are not immediately good at. The aim should be exploration, not pressure to excel at everything."],
  [/what skills can people learn from watching videos/i, "Videos are useful for skills that can be shown step by step, such as basic cooking, drawing techniques or using a computer program. A learner can pause, replay a difficult part and compare it with their own attempt. They are less effective when someone needs personal feedback or safety supervision, so a video is often a helpful starting point rather than a complete substitute for practice."],
  [/how can parents help children develop a love of reading/i, "Parents can make books easy to reach, read aloud together and let children choose subjects they are genuinely curious about. A regular bedtime story can make reading feel like time together instead of homework. It is also useful for children to see adults reading for enjoyment. If every book is followed by a test, children may focus on getting the answer right rather than enjoying the story."],
  [/why do you think parents still make plans for their children nowadays/i, "Parents make plans because they want to give their children security and access to opportunities, especially around education and health. They may arrange lessons or save for university because those decisions involve time and money. The risk is planning every hour and leaving no room for the child's own interests. Support is helpful, but children also need a say in choices that shape their lives."],
  [/what can teachers do to stop students relying too much on ai/i, "Teachers can design tasks that require students to explain their reasoning, discuss sources and show how they reached an answer. In a mathematics assignment, for example, students could compare an AI-generated solution with their own working and identify an error. Clear rules about when tools are allowed also help. Simply banning AI may be hard to enforce and misses a chance to teach responsible use."],
  [/why do some people have a better memory than others/i, "Memory differs because of attention, sleep, practice and the way people connect new information with what they already know. A student who reviews a formula by using it in several problems may remember it better than someone who reads it once. There are natural differences too, but habits matter. Good memory is not only a fixed talent; people can improve how they learn and recall."],
  [/what do you think about some countries banning children from using social media/i, "A complete ban may protect younger children from harmful content and pressure to compare themselves with others, but it is difficult to enforce and may stop them learning how to use social media safely. I would prefer age-appropriate limits, parental guidance and stronger privacy settings. Schools can teach digital judgement as well. Children need protection, but they also need to build responsible habits gradually."],
  [/when would parents feel proud of their children/i, "Parents may feel proud when children make a sustained effort, treat others well or handle a setback with more independence. Good grades can be one reason, but they are not the only measure of progress. A child who admits a mistake and tries to fix it may show maturity that a test score cannot capture. Pride is strongest when it recognises the person, not just the result."],
  [/what do you think about children working hard just for grades/i, "Working hard can help children learn, but doing it only for grades may make them anxious and less curious. A student might memorise a method for an exam without understanding when it is useful. Teachers and parents should recognise improvement and clear thinking as well as marks. Grades matter for some opportunities, but they should be feedback rather than a child's only reason to study."],
  [/what is the right age for a child to learn drawing|why do most children draw more often than adults do/i, "There is no single right age; children can start drawing as soon as they enjoy holding a crayon and making marks. They often draw more than adults because they have more unstructured time and worry less about producing something impressive. A relaxed activity lets them experiment with shapes and stories. Adults can enjoy drawing too if they treat it as play rather than judge every result."],
  [/what jobs do children want to do when they grow up/i, "Children often mention visible jobs such as doctor, teacher, police officer or athlete because they have seen those roles in daily life or on television. Their answers can change as they meet new people and learn what a job involves. I think adults should take the interest seriously without asking a child to decide too early. Curiosity is more useful than a fixed career plan at that age."],
  [/what are the advantages and disadvantages of being a famous child|what can children do with their fame/i, "Being famous as a child can bring opportunities, such as meeting interesting people or earning money, but it also puts private life under public attention before the child is mature enough to manage it. Adults may benefit financially from the child's image, so protection and informed consent matter. Fame should never take priority over education, friendships and the child's right to change their mind later."],
  [/how do children become smart at school/i, "Children develop their abilities when lessons match their level, teachers give useful feedback and they have room to ask questions. A student may understand a difficult idea after seeing it explained in a different way or applying it to a small project. Practice matters, but so does confidence to make mistakes. Schools should help each child find strengths rather than label some as naturally smart."],
  [/what difficulties do people face when learning a language|what.s the best way to learn a language|what are the advantages and disadvantages of learning a language/i, "A common difficulty is understanding grammar and vocabulary while also speaking quickly enough to join a real conversation. Regular practice helps more than memorising long lists: learners can listen to short material, note useful phrases and then use them with another person. Progress takes time, and embarrassment can discourage people. A class or language partner gives feedback, while independent study lets them practise at their own pace."],
  [/when do most children begin to have their own opinions|whose opinions are more important to children/i, "Children begin expressing preferences quite early, but their opinions become more considered as they gain experience and learn to explain their reasons. Parents often shape early choices because they are responsible for safety, while teachers influence children through school and subject knowledge. Neither should automatically decide everything. Adults can listen, offer context and let children make age-appropriate choices so they learn to think independently."],
  [/why is self-discipline important when learning a new skill/i, "Self-discipline helps because progress usually comes from short, regular practice rather than one intense session. Someone learning an instrument or a language may feel awkward at first, but a routine makes it easier to continue through that stage. It also helps people notice whether a method is working. Discipline should not mean practising without rest; a realistic schedule is more sustainable."],
  [/what kinds of rules do schools in china have/i, "Schools in China commonly set rules about attendance, uniforms, classroom behaviour and phone use. Some also set limits on homework or require students to take part in morning exercises. The details vary by school, but the rules are meant to keep lessons orderly and make expectations clear. They work best when students understand the reason behind them, rather than following them only to avoid punishment."],
  [/how can parents and teachers help children understand and follow rules/i, "They should explain the reason for a rule and apply it consistently. For example, asking children to put phones away is easier to accept when adults explain how notifications interrupt a lesson and follow the same expectation themselves. Parents and teachers can also praise a child for making the right choice, instead of relying only on punishment when something goes wrong."],
  [/why are some teachers.? classes boring/i, "A class can feel boring when students spend most of the time copying notes and never see how the material connects with anything real. A long explanation with no questions also makes it hard to stay involved. In mathematics, even a short example showing where an idea is used can make a lesson more interesting. The topic matters, but the way it is taught matters just as much."],
  [/what are the qualities of being a good teacher/i, "A good teacher explains ideas clearly, notices when students are confused and gives feedback they can act on. I remember teachers who broke a difficult problem into smaller steps instead of simply repeating the answer. Patience matters, but so does setting high expectations; students learn more when they feel supported and are still encouraged to think for themselves."],
  [/what group tasks are there in schools/i, "Students often work together on presentations, science projects, class discussions and performances. A project might involve one person finding information, another checking the data and someone else presenting the result. These tasks show students how to share responsibility and combine different strengths. They work best when everyone has a clear role, so one confident student does not end up doing everything."],
  [/what advantages are there for students experiencing teamwork at school/i, "Teamwork gives students practice explaining their ideas, listening to different views and dividing up a task. For example, in a group presentation, one student may be good at research while another is more confident speaking to the class. They learn from each other and get a result they could not produce as easily alone. Teachers still need to make sure each student contributes."],
  [/what sports matches are suitable for children to attend/i, "Local matches with a relaxed atmosphere are usually a good choice, such as school basketball or a family-friendly football game. They are easier for children to follow than a very long event, and the venue is less overwhelming. Parents should check crowd safety and make sure the child is interested; the aim is to enjoy the game, not to force them to sit through it."],
  [/what are the differences between watching movies at home and in a cinema/i, "At home, people can pause a film, choose when to watch and avoid the cost of tickets and snacks. A cinema offers a larger screen and stronger sound, which can make an action film feel more absorbing. I tend to watch ordinary films at home, but I go to the cinema for something visually impressive. The better option depends on the film and the company."],
  [/what kind of photos do people often take/i, "People often take photos of family and friends, meals, pets and places they visit. These subjects are part of daily life, so the pictures become a record of relationships as well as events. Phones have also made it easy to take photos of small things people find beautiful or funny. I think the most meaningful pictures are usually the ones that remind someone of a particular moment."],
  [/why are some people keen on taking photos/i, "Some people enjoy photography as a creative hobby: they pay attention to light, colour and the way a scene is framed. Others mainly take pictures to keep memories or share what they are doing with friends. For example, a family may take the same group photo at every holiday gathering. The reasons differ, but a photo lets people return to a moment they would otherwise remember only vaguely."],
  [/why do tourists like taking photos while they are travelling/i, "Photos help tourists keep a record of places and share the experience with people who were not there. A picture of a street market or mountain view can bring back small details more vividly than a written note. Social media encourages people to take even more pictures, though sometimes they focus on the camera instead of the place. A few photos are a useful memory; taking them constantly can interrupt the visit."],
  [/where can (?:young )?children make new friends/i, "School is the obvious place, but the easiest friendships often start around a shared activity. A playground, sports club or art class gives children something to talk about before they know one another well. For instance, two children waiting to use the same swing may start playing together. Seeing each other regularly helps that first conversation turn into trust; adults can create the setting, but they do not need to manage every interaction."],
  [/how important is childhood friendship/i, "It is very important because friends give children a safe place to practise sharing, solving disagreements and being themselves. A classmate who sits with a child on the first day of school can make an unfamiliar setting feel manageable. Those early friendships may not all last, but learning how to build and repair them is useful well into adulthood."],
  [/has technology changed people.s friendships/i, "Yes, it has made it much easier to stay in touch when friends study or work in different places. At the same time, a stream of short updates can create a feeling of closeness without much real conversation. I keep up with classmates in group chats, but I would still call a close friend when something important happens. It is a useful channel, not a substitute for attention."],
  [/what positive and negative impact do mobile phones have on friendship/i, "Phones make it easy to share plans and check in, especially when friends are busy or live far apart. The downside is that quick messages can replace a proper conversation, and people may feel ignored when someone keeps looking at a screen. I use group chats for everyday updates, but for anything personal I prefer to talk directly."],
  [/how might society change.*friendship.*social bonds|if friendship and other social bonds were stronger/i, "Everyday problems might be handled earlier and with less formality. In a large apartment block, neighbours who recognise one another could check on an older resident, share a small errand or help a parent in a busy week. That could reduce isolation and take pressure off some services. The qualification is that close groups can shut out newcomers, so stronger ties would make a community warmer, but not automatically more inclusive."],
  [/do people.s movie preferences change as they get older|movie preferences change.*older/i, "Yes, they often do, partly because people's routines and responsibilities change. Someone with young children may choose a shorter, familiar film, while a student might still enjoy a long action film with friends. Age is not the only influence, though; personality, culture and who someone watches with matter as well. I think our tastes shift gradually as our lives change, rather than on a particular birthday."],
  [/should documentary films be completely truthful|documentaries.*completely truthful/i, "They should be accurate about facts, but that does not mean a documentary can show every side of a subject in equal detail. A director still chooses which interviews and scenes to include, so the editing should be transparent and avoid creating a misleading impression. For example, a short film can explain that an interview represents one person's experience, not an entire community. A clear point of view is acceptable; distorting evidence is not."],
];

function partThreeAnswer(topic, question, index) {
  const current = question.toLowerCase().replace(/[?？]+$/, "").trim();
  const followUp = /^(why|why or why not|how|what about|and why)$/.test(current) && index > 0;
  const context = `${followUp ? topic.questions[index - 1] : current} ${current}`.toLowerCase();
  const specific = partThreeSpecific.find(([pattern]) => pattern.test(context))?.[1];
  if (specific) return specific;
  const profileText = followUp ? context : current;
  const rankedProfiles = partThreeProfiles.map(([pattern, profile]) => {
    const match = pattern.exec(profileText);
    const score = match ? match[0].replace(/[^a-z]/gi, "").length : 0;
    return { profile, score };
  }).sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score);
  const p = rankedProfiles[0]?.score ? rankedProfiles[0].profile
    : partThreeProfiles.find(([pattern]) => pattern.test(`${topic.prompt || ""} ${topic.title}`))?.[1] || p3Fallback;
  let direct = "";
  if (!followUp && /^should\b/.test(current)) {
    direct = /not be allowed|\bban\b|\bprohibit\b|under no circumstances/.test(current)
      ? "No, a blanket ban would be too strict."
      : "Yes, I think so, provided the approach is practical and fair.";
  } else if (!followUp && /^(do|does|did|is|are|can|could|will|would)\b/.test(current)) {
    const variation = [...`${topic.title}${current}`].reduce((sum, character) => sum + character.charCodeAt(0), 0) % 3;
    direct = ["Generally, yes, although it depends on the situation.", "I would say so, with a few exceptions.", "On balance, yes, but the details matter."][variation];
  } else if (!followUp && /\b(?:difference|different|compare|which .*better|prefer .* or)\b/.test(current)) {
    direct = "There is not one choice that suits everyone; the main difference is what people need from it.";
  }
  let answer = `${direct} ${p.reason} ${p.example} ${p.balance}`.trim();
  if (countWords(answer) < 55) answer += " In the end, the details of how it works matter more than the idea on its own.";
  return answer;
}
